#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d8c6c93-9d15-5482-8130-25bb9cabf008" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions to Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c271d2f6-11aa-5258-b730-953ddc737b00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c271d2f6-11aa-5258-b730-953ddc737b00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}(11)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256455} :answer-field/id #uuid "9e3000f4-f290-5e72-a98c-b7f4789a1dc9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256454}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "272af2d8-c0a8-526a-8af7-4492ae771147" :question/math-academy-id "q-100282" :question/problem "Find the exact solution of the equation ${e}^{t}=11.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{t} & =11 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{t}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nt=\\operatorname{ln}(11)\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $t=\\operatorname{ln}(11).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256736 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91bf0b47-327f-5b82-a87a-307b7eda9e1c" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Arithmetic Series Using Sigma Notation when Not All Terms are Shown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c3eb03e6-7355-530b-a62a-5ab90976e93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{9}(\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c3eb03e6-7355-530b-a62a-5ab90976e93d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{9}(\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256738} :answer-field/id #uuid "49686207-7b26-5609-809c-0d7218e5e071" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256737}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4f95e576-a69f-50d3-a4ad-5ca5441cb0c4" :question/math-academy-id "q-107659" :question/problem "Express the following arithmetic series using sigma notation.\n\n$$\n2+\\frac{1}{4}-\\frac{3}{2}+…-\\frac{41}{4}-12\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The first term ${a}_{1}=2$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}-2 \\\\  & =-\\frac{7}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =2+(n-1)(-\\frac{7}{4}) \\\\  & =2-\\frac{7}{4}n+\\frac{7}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe don't know the index of the last term, but we can find it using the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-12 \\\\ \\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n & =-12 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4}n & =\\frac{15}{4}+12 \\\\ \\frac{7}{4}n & =\\frac{63}{4} \\\\ n & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $9$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=9.$ So, the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{9}(\\frac{15}{4}-\\frac{7}{4}n)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256739 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91bf0b47-327f-5b82-a87a-307b7eda9e1c" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Arithmetic Series Using Sigma Notation when Not All Terms are Shown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdb1809c-a2b2-5756-af00-14adab920c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{17}(-7n+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdb1809c-a2b2-5756-af00-14adab920c87" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{17}(-7n+3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256741} :answer-field/id #uuid "88aca771-c3f9-5d92-9c15-d20b0f5a4ec7" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256740}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "114b36be-4de0-5cb7-978d-00c318e1a7d2" :question/math-academy-id "q-107668" :question/problem "Express the following arithmetic series using sigma notation.\n\n$$\n-4-11-18+…-109-116\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The first term ${a}_{1}=-4$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-11-(-4) \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-4+(n-1)(-7) \\\\  & =-4-7n+7 \\\\  & =-7n+3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe don't know the index of the last term, but we can find it using the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-116 \\\\ -7n+3 & =-116 \\\\ -7n & =-119 \\\\ n & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $17$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=17.$ So, the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{17}(-7n+3)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "829ac8f1-7ade-5dd9-9f2f-78234a57a257" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given the First and Last Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "37cfaf94-1a46-5072-bebb-e1a08259948a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "616" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256747}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "37cfaf94-1a46-5072-bebb-e1a08259948a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "616" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256747} :answer-field/id #uuid "8532ae84-3559-5cd7-b556-eff814ccedbd" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256746}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c01979e1-2f0d-57eb-bf22-2ecf15ad2571" :question/math-academy-id "q-107749" :question/problem "Find the sum of the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n5+11+17+23+⋯+83\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the total number of terms.\n\nFor our series, we have ${a}_{1}=5$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =11-5 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =5+(n-1)(6) \\\\  & =5+6n-6 \\\\  & =6n-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we know, the last term is ${a}_{N}=83.$ Putting this value in the formula for the $n$th term obtained above, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =6N-1 \\\\ 83 & =6N-1 \\\\ 84 & =6N \\\\ N & =14\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting ${a}_{1}=5$, ${a}_{14}=83$, and $N=14$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{14} & =\\frac{14}{2}(5+83) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 88 \\\\  & =616\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256748 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "829ac8f1-7ade-5dd9-9f2f-78234a57a257" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given the First and Last Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f702305-766f-5dd0-8437-9e8a07a18a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\,364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0f702305-766f-5dd0-8437-9e8a07a18a6f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8\\,364" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256750} :answer-field/id #uuid "cdd765af-f333-566b-8af6-2696251ec62c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256749}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "06cac020-1582-549c-9faf-4dee975a31ad" :question/math-academy-id "q-107751" :question/problem "Find the sum of the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n(-14)+(-20)+(-26)+(-32)+⋯+(-314)\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the total number of terms.\n\nFor our series, we have ${a}_{1}=-14$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-20-(-14) \\\\  & =-6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =-14+(n-1)(-6) \\\\  & =-14-6n+6 \\\\  & =-6n-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs we know, the last term is ${a}_{N}=-314.$ Putting this value in the formula for the $n$th term obtained above, we get:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & =-6N-8 \\\\ -314 & =-6N-8 \\\\ -306 & =-6N \\\\ N & =51\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting ${a}_{1}=-14$, ${a}_{51}=-314$, and $N=51$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{51} & =\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}) \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{2}\\cdot (-14-314) \\\\  & =\\frac{51}{2}\\cdot (-328) \\\\  & =-8\\,364\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256751 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d65ee5e-2211-5822-a99a-c49dfe4d2bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given the First Term and the Total Number of Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "af802da0-192c-5cc1-a289-cc9a48a4a188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,020" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256753}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "af802da0-192c-5cc1-a289-cc9a48a4a188" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,020" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256753} :answer-field/id #uuid "894a8a89-af82-5b7b-828c-769b05f9756a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256752}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "34c81d61-3e3f-5632-9eae-10ea56529564" :question/math-academy-id "q-107760" :question/problem "Calculate the sum of the first $20$ terms of the following arithmetic series. \n\n$$\n13+17+21+⋯\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nWe are given the total number of terms $N=20$ and the first term is ${a}_{1}=13.$ Now, the common difference $d$ can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =17-13 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=13$, $d=4$, and $N=20$ into the above formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{20} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2}(2\\cdot 13+(20-1)\\cdot 4) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2}\\cdot (26+76) \\\\  & =\\frac{20}{2}\\cdot 102 \\\\  & =10\\cdot 102 \\\\  & =1\\,020\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7d65ee5e-2211-5822-a99a-c49dfe4d2bbc" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given the First Term and the Total Number of Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cdb382b0-20e2-5cac-aa00-9a26b5d81f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256756}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cdb382b0-20e2-5cac-aa00-9a26b5d81f32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "48" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256756} :answer-field/id #uuid "81f67404-4664-57c5-a7ff-495c9b6fa304" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256755}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e928ff1c-3648-56d1-a33b-01de56cefa49" :question/math-academy-id "q-107794" :question/problem "Calculate the sum of the first $15$ terms of the following arithmetic series.\n\n$$\n-1+(-\\frac{2}{5})+\\frac{1}{5}+\\frac{4}{5}+⋯\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nWe are given the total number of terms $N=15$ and the first term is ${a}_{1}=-1.$ Now, the common difference $d$ can be found as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\frac{2}{5}-(-1) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=-1$, $d=\\frac{3}{5}$, and $N=15$ into the above formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{15} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}(2(-1)+(15-1)\\cdot \\frac{3}{5}) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot (-2+8.4) \\\\  & =7.5\\cdot 6.4 \\\\  & =48\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256789 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fa5bdf9-4b27-5565-9ee1-ecb9f9db4791" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Units of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "54f6f675-87c1-50bf-ae96-b0ca58aac52e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Visitors per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256791}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "54f6f675-87c1-50bf-ae96-b0ca58aac52e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Visitors per hour" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256791} :answer-field/id #uuid "316d870c-3af4-574b-8b2e-fc81731c9bcd" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256790}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c773c57f-ca50-5bf6-b884-84eac176a3dd" :question/math-academy-id "q-110040" :question/problem "A website receives $v(h)$ visits every $h$ hours. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nWrite the units in the form \"[output units] per [input unit]\"." :question/worked-solution "We have that the time $h$ is measured in hours and the number of visits is measured in visitors. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}h: & \\text{hours} \\\\ v(h): & \\text{visitors}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}$ measures a change in $v$ over a change in $h.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}: & \\frac{\\text{visitors}}{\\text{hours}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}h}$ is measured in \"visitors per hour \"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "655f4b04-4eec-5386-b349-7c26d6c6c586" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "05b767e5-a9aa-524d-91a0-5c0860dc8bcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{5}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256805}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "05b767e5-a9aa-524d-91a0-5c0860dc8bcb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{5}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256805} :answer-field/id #uuid "ac18b7f5-d430-595d-868a-e6af9ce80ab6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256804}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dbfdc4aa-e0a1-5cb8-a5f8-802705527624" :question/math-academy-id "q-110195" :question/problem "The geometric series $1+5+25+125+625$ can be written using sigma notation as\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=1$ and $N=5.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we calculate the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{5}{1}=5\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressing the geometric series given in sigma notation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+5+25+125+625 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}1\\cdot {5}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{5}{5}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256806 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "655f4b04-4eec-5386-b349-7c26d6c6c586" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "faceb76e-10b9-512e-9e16-11ad94852b3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256808}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "faceb76e-10b9-512e-9e16-11ad94852b3a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256808} :answer-field/id #uuid "54b35472-4953-517f-92fe-062bfd8fcbd6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256807}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8349b03f-f80a-5400-be8a-3eaa57417e1a" :question/math-academy-id "q-110200" :question/problem "The geometric series $2+8+32+128$ can be written using sigma notation as\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=2$ and $N=4.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we calculate the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{8}{2}=4\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressing the geometric series given in sigma notation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2+8+32+128 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\cdot {4}^{n-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e44759-4db8-57e3-bfb4-ce026d4d73b2" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series Using Sigma Notation by Calculating the Number of Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "710cb0c9-4c60-59d5-82d5-d28bbd49b82c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\,401\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256811}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "710cb0c9-4c60-59d5-82d5-d28bbd49b82c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\,401\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256811} :answer-field/id #uuid "81090203-c244-5ee4-b79f-32c5ec061bb9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256810}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "24124fc9-7f50-533d-9cb7-ef782b3922cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-110212" :question/problem "The geometric series $2\\,401+343+⋯+7$  can be expressed using sigma notation as\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=2\\,401.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio of the consecutive terms ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{343}{2\\,401}=\\frac{1}{7}\n$$\n\nNow we must determine $N$ (the index of the last term). To do this, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1} \\\\ 7 & =2\\,401\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{N-1} \\\\ \\frac{7}{2\\,401} & ={(\\frac{1}{7})}^{N-1} \\\\ \\frac{1}{343} & =\\frac{1}{{7}^{N-1}} \\\\ 343 & ={7}^{N-1} \\\\ {7}^{3} & ={7}^{N-1} \\\\ 3 & =N-1 \\\\ N & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing the geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,401+343+⋯+7 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}2\\,401\\cdot {(\\frac{1}{7})}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256821 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd612e5f-cc72-5df4-94da-e290473ed0a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of a Geometric Series Given With a Zero Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7fd952d3-2187-57f2-a6ea-3287343f7b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{242}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7fd952d3-2187-57f2-a6ea-3287343f7b91" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{242}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256823} :answer-field/id #uuid "e145e81e-6d32-5816-8b4d-7fbdfeb2d2d2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256822}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "032226b5-f50b-5569-a40d-8825ca8a0e5f" :question/math-academy-id "q-110335" :question/problem "Evaluate the geometric series ${\\sum }_{n=0}^{4}2{(3)}^{n-1}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. Notice that the index starts from $n=0$ instead of $n=1.$\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{0}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=3.$ Also, note that $N=5,$ since there are $5$ terms in total.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2{(3)}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nand so the first term ${a}_{0}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =2{(3)}^{0-1} \\\\  & =2{(3)}^{-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{3},r=3$ and $N=5$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{5} & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{3}^{5})}{(1-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{3}^{5})}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-242) \\\\  & =\\frac{242}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256854 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb183013-a42d-50a3-a1b8-cd8501781075" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial With Multiple Variables as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9ed374db-f5a0-53d7-a45d-279793672c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})(r+5s)(r-5s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256856}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9ed374db-f5a0-53d7-a45d-279793672c5b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})(r+5s)(r-5s)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256856} :answer-field/id #uuid "f169cd56-1140-5eae-bf99-095fbc578cf5" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256855}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "79c6e0be-06e2-559d-80d7-97545e89753f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112042" :question/problem "Factor the expression ${r}^{4}-(5s)^{4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{r}^{4}-(5s)^{4}={({r}^{2})}^{2}-{((5s)^{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{({r}^{2})}^{2}-{((5s)^{2})}^{2} & =({r}^{2}+(5s)^{2})({r}^{2}-(5s)^{2}) \\\\  & =({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})({r}^{2}-(5s)^{2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})({r}^{2}-(5s)^{2}) & =({r}^{2}+25{s}^{2})(r+5s)(r-5s)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb183013-a42d-50a3-a1b8-cd8501781075" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial With Multiple Variables as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1361249a-36d7-57c4-bdd3-eb4790195c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})(j+3k)(j-3k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256862}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1361249a-36d7-57c4-bdd3-eb4790195c0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})(j+3k)(j-3k)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256862} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7ac8d6c-480e-527d-8148-9e8ad4d8a254" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256861}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b9eff020-73a6-55b5-a5ce-93f7544df575" :question/math-academy-id "q-112045" :question/problem "Factor the expression ${j}^{4}-81{k}^{4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{j}^{4}-81{k}^{4}={({j}^{2})}^{2}-{(9{k}^{2})}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{({j}^{2})}^{2}-{(9{k}^{2})}^{2}=({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})({j}^{2}-9{k}^{2})\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares:\n \n\n$$\n{j}^{2}-9{k}^{2}={j}^{2}-{(3k)}^{2}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})({j}^{2}-9{k}^{2}) & =({j}^{2}+9{k}^{2})(j+3k)(j-3k)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256863 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7df31c9-b27a-5ce2-93c8-178ba96da000" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7d43a382-c034-5630-8dba-d0491c7e917d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256865}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7d43a382-c034-5630-8dba-d0491c7e917d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256865} :answer-field/id #uuid "57fa2f2d-5fc0-5725-9f07-9fc0207ec675" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256864}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a6d442f-4688-5203-a723-98f8e649492f" :question/math-academy-id "q-112382" :question/problem "After taking a pill containing $300$ milligrams of a particular drug, the quantity of the drug in a patient's body decreases at a rate of $c(t)=300\\cdot {2}^{-0.7t}$ milligrams per hour, where $t$ is the time, in hours, since the patient consumed the pill. What will be the rate of decrease after $5$ hours? Round your answer to the nearest integer.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Since we want to determine the rate of decrease in the quantity of the drug in the patient's body after $5$ hours, we have $t=5.$\n\nThus, the rate of decrease in the quantity of the drug after $5$ hours will be\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}c(5) & =300\\cdot {2}^{-0.7(5)} \\\\  & =300\\cdot {2}^{-3.5} \\\\  & \\approx 27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the rate of decrease will be $27$ milligrams per hour."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256866 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69d547a5-adac-5547-ba62-a093c4262da3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H'(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69d547a5-adac-5547-ba62-a093c4262da3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "H'(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256868} :answer-field/id #uuid "cbf5d2b0-c275-5a08-a54b-aa11285096a1" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256867}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3f4c5624-08a5-5d28-9dbb-7ddefb779501" :question/math-academy-id "q-112509" :question/problem "Joseph is measuring the height reached by his lime tree by recording the following two numbers for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in years, since he planted the tree:\n\n- $H(t)$ - The tree's height, in centimeters\n\n- $r(t)$ - the rate at which the tree's height is increasing.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $H(t)$ and $r(t)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $H(t)$ gives the tree's height after $t$ years. Then,\n\n$$\n{H}^{'}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}H(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }H}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${H}^{'}(t)$ gives the rate at which the height of the tree increases at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $H(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${H}^{'}(t)=r(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "070582c7-9118-59ae-8fb8-cefbfc2e8342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256943}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "070582c7-9118-59ae-8fb8-cefbfc2e8342" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256943} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d03e6ea-0b8d-5cd4-ad47-a3accdda2667" :answer-field/key "x" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256942}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c37cf0e8-bbca-5ef1-ad17-b5e71311507a" :question/math-academy-id "q-113523" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+3x-3}.$\n\n$x =$: {{x}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{2x+4}{{x}^{3}-{x}^{2}+3x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2)}{{x}^{2}(x-1)+3(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(x+2)}{({x}^{2}+3)(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n({x}^{2}+3)(x-1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-1=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation ${x}^{2}+3=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the only vertical asymptote is $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256944 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5b59731b-ccb9-53c5-82cf-caaaeebe66fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256946}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5b59731b-ccb9-53c5-82cf-caaaeebe66fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256946} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ce9b096-dc68-52ed-9491-356c80fb6f40" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256945}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "89758aa9-2520-5f08-bcf3-4dd38de13609" :question/math-academy-id "q-113547" :question/problem "Find all of the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x+1}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No vertical asymptotes\"." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{3}+{x}^{2}+x+1} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}(x+1)+(x+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x+1}{(x+1)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{x+1}}{\\cancel{(x+1)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ does not have any vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b0b57da-f9af-5fab-a337-51ee4cb28f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91ab451d-c25c-5f68-b886-6bf28b6f2808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256949}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91ab451d-c25c-5f68-b886-6bf28b6f2808" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256949} :answer-field/id #uuid "1ca49ee6-7b3e-50f6-b6b8-2580dda21a0b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256948}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c1d02b50-c109-51fe-8f01-39a10ce1834c" :question/math-academy-id "q-113550" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of the function $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No vertical asymptotes\"." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-3x}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}+x-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x-3)}{{x}^{2}(x-3)+(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x(x-3)}{(x-3)({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x\\cancel{(x-3)}}{\\cancel{(x-3)}({x}^{2}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{x}{{x}^{2}+1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero:\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}+1=0\n$$\n\nThis equation does not have any real solutions. Therefore, $f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257059 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7b17a61-b225-5493-98b0-58d15217aa31" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations by Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bb58f1b-ad77-5cae-90c8-ea9646ffaddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1bb58f1b-ad77-5cae-90c8-ea9646ffaddb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257061} :answer-field/id #uuid "8af7d4f7-3b32-5c18-a8eb-978ee876b78b" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257060} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61aec294-9680-5eee-a1e6-d30e05f7dc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257063}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "61aec294-9680-5eee-a1e6-d30e05f7dc5f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257063} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea22afb8-c570-5fdb-8e76-29ae379540b9" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257062} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e7fce91d-b78d-5daa-9eed-3799c8e24a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257065}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7fce91d-b78d-5daa-9eed-3799c8e24a06" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257065} :answer-field/id #uuid "b1df33b5-cfb0-5844-9815-e3e3017e433a" :answer-field/key "root-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257064}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a4c3b57b-4f7f-5532-bd9e-228bcbf5fb45" :question/math-academy-id "q-114145" :question/problem "Solve ${m}^{3}-3{m}^{2}-2m+6=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{3}-3{m}^{2}-2m+6 & = \\\\ ({m}^{3}-3{m}^{2})-(2m-6) & = \\\\ {m}^{2}(m-3)-2(m-3) & = \\\\ (m-3)\\cdot {m}^{2}-(m-3)\\cdot 2 & = \\\\ (m-3)({m}^{2}-2) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(m-3)({m}^{2}-2)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m-3 & =0 \\\\ m & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({m}^{2}-2)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{2}-2 & =0 \\\\ {m}^{2} & =2 \\\\ m & =\\pm \\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $m=3,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257066 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01d58dfd-315a-5a4f-ae16-1db04010169a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations With Negative Leading Coefficients by Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3d4fdd28-7877-56a4-bc70-4e8b10186cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257068}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3d4fdd28-7877-56a4-bc70-4e8b10186cde" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257068} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a584ce7-9315-5a23-b80e-2605fbf76445" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257067} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da0fccf8-c278-5e86-9eed-d7be58b2c9e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257070}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "da0fccf8-c278-5e86-9eed-d7be58b2c9e8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257070} :answer-field/id #uuid "641d8c63-1219-58d4-8e45-b974fdb2de65" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257069} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0628c3c7-35d3-534e-b42d-73e3db0b2732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0628c3c7-35d3-534e-b42d-73e3db0b2732" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257072} :answer-field/id #uuid "bca6de13-e11f-58a0-a5da-a5be6ab24e19" :answer-field/key "root-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257071}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a3c48b1d-9c5d-5b27-89de-0584bf82834b" :question/math-academy-id "q-114205" :question/problem "Solve $-18{v}^{3}-63{v}^{2}+2v+7=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$ to make the coefficient of the cubic term positive. This gives\n\n$$\n18{v}^{3}+63{v}^{2}-2v-7=0.\n$$\n\nWe then proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}18{v}^{3}+63{v}^{2}-2v-7 & = \\\\ 9{v}^{2}(2v+7)-1(2v+7) & = \\\\ (9{v}^{2}-1)(2v+7) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(9{v}^{2}-1)(2v+7)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(9{v}^{2}-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{v}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{9} \\\\ v & =\\pm \\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2v+7)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2v & =-7 \\\\ v & =-\\frac{7}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $v=-\\frac{7}{2},\\,\\pm \\frac{1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257076 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "01d58dfd-315a-5a4f-ae16-1db04010169a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations With Negative Leading Coefficients by Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e472f6af-282c-5f81-8a6f-aa842163264c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257078}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e472f6af-282c-5f81-8a6f-aa842163264c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257078} :answer-field/id #uuid "f6d70c21-c5c7-5a91-ad70-3da57be7affc" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257077} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "38e65078-5b50-50dd-8580-957b11fe5c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257080}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "38e65078-5b50-50dd-8580-957b11fe5c19" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257080} :answer-field/id #uuid "e4280d61-9ff1-5d9c-a021-b2720a3231c9" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257079} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01c18fee-8168-5be8-b443-ccc5b402231c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257082}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "01c18fee-8168-5be8-b443-ccc5b402231c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257082} :answer-field/id #uuid "771c2df9-b495-5230-a083-074b60a82938" :answer-field/key "root-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257081}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "62a71b17-2204-59c1-8d38-eaaccf24e868" :question/math-academy-id "q-114210" :question/problem "Solve $-{m}^{3}-3{m}^{2}+25m+75=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we multiply the equation by $-1$ to make the coefficient of the cubic term positive. This gives\n\n$$\n{m}^{3}+3{m}^{2}-25m-75=0.\n$$\n\nWe then proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{m}^{3}+3{m}^{2}-25m-75 & = \\\\ ({m}^{3}+3{m}^{2})+(-25m-75) & = \\\\ {m}^{2}(m+3)-25(m+3) & = \\\\ ({m}^{2}-25)(m+3) & = \\\\ (m+5)(m-5)(m+3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(m+5)(m-5)(m+3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m+5)$ equal to $0,$ we get $m=-5.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m-5)$ equal to $0,$ we get $m=5.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(m+3)$ equal to $0,$ we get $m=-3.$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $m=-3,\\,\\pm 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257086 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6158975-c81e-5621-aa39-3f2dc728f107" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations by Manipulating the Equation and Then Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8a955969-f466-516a-b8cd-ae2e2f604e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257088}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a955969-f466-516a-b8cd-ae2e2f604e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257088} :answer-field/id #uuid "92d6932f-6710-5414-aa49-fb7f88f2cad2" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257087} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fedf16c4-e5bf-528a-a399-52ce2c132d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257090}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fedf16c4-e5bf-528a-a399-52ce2c132d24" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac34" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257090} :answer-field/id #uuid "6674e455-320b-5e9c-9b03-6e11a638031c" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257089} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "59746aed-1db5-5d6b-ae53-1f14566b0b32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257092}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "59746aed-1db5-5d6b-ae53-1f14566b0b32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257092} :answer-field/id #uuid "e8c7296b-a4ad-5a93-9daf-34aeb8afc1c2" :answer-field/key "root-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257091}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a79ebad8-83ac-5875-bf1f-d77b87643be1" :question/math-academy-id "q-114339" :question/problem "Solve $8{u}^{3}-6{u}^{2}-72u+54=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" :question/worked-solution "Since we have an overall common factor of $2$, we start by dividing our equation through by $2.$ This gives \n\n$$\n4{u}^{3}-3{u}^{2}-36u+27=0.\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4{u}^{3}-3{u}^{2}-36u+27 & = \\\\ (4{u}^{3}-3{u}^{2})-(36u-27) & = \\\\ {u}^{2}(4u-3)-9(4u-3) & = \\\\ (4u-3)\\cdot {u}^{2}-(4u-3)\\cdot 9 & = \\\\ (4u-3)({u}^{2}-9) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve:\n\n- Setting the factor $(4u-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4u-3 & =0 \\\\ 4u & =3 \\\\ u & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({u}^{2}-9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{2}-9 & =0 \\\\ {u}^{2} & =9 \\\\ u & =\\pm 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the solutions to the given equation are $\\frac{3}{4}$ and $\\pm 3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257131 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dff5b7c-544a-5fda-a175-51ce4723a135" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Greater Than or Equal To\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fa84f31-40f0-5b3c-8b32-5a84fa150210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257133}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fa84f31-40f0-5b3c-8b32-5a84fa150210" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257133} :answer-field/id #uuid "b53af6e4-df94-59e3-bb64-b0a27745178d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257132}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7a8e5c30-d271-53bb-8418-8317e11f8e2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-115324" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/8c266bd4087fbfad0f81de882dc72cf03e81d420897f1f1b1a3b430827a77f2a.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $(x+2)(x-2)\\ge 0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n(x+2)(x-2)\\ge 0\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=(x+2)(x-2)\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/1b6d9689cf4ac9d36507b4fc3ba0ea77ac34ee8a4cf5cd28a5592c834f48ecec.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -2$ or $x\\ge 2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257158 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0312e17-e90a-5cec-a973-4fe0cd198064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-1,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257160}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0312e17-e90a-5cec-a973-4fe0cd198064" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-1,5]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257160} :answer-field/id #uuid "b5524af5-d05e-5356-866e-1d3ddab40d3c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257159}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e171ae29-e8a4-5154-8cf5-f799b433e6ca" :question/math-academy-id "q-116199" :question/problem "Find the solution of $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x\\le x+\\frac{5}{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x & \\le x+\\frac{5}{2} \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-2x-\\frac{5}{2} & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}({x}^{2}-4x-5) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(x+1)(x-5) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+1)(x-5) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+1)(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=-1$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-1),$ $(-1,5)$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-1)$ | $(-1,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+1$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+1)(x-5)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+1)(x-5)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+1)(x-5)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-1,5).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-1$ and $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}-x\\le x+\\frac{5}{2}$ for all $x\\in [-1,5].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "dc018e0e-60bc-59bd-a3f6-8ae2b68f8cfb" :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Inequality by Rearranging Terms and Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "001edcb1-c7f0-5737-ba45-b733679d2763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-5,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257163}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "001edcb1-c7f0-5737-ba45-b733679d2763" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [-5,4]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257163} :answer-field/id #uuid "7eed7fe2-f8ea-5f28-83ae-f1607966dce1" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257162}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "31b9e724-be24-5c0d-8724-b3dd3a9b5157" :question/math-academy-id "q-116201" :question/problem "Find the solution of $\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{10}x\\le 2.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rewrite the given inequality so that the right-hand side equals zero. Then, we factor the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{10}x & \\le 2 \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{10}x-2 & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}({x}^{2}+x-20) & \\le 0 \\\\ \\frac{1}{10}(x+5)(x-4) & \\le 0 \\\\ (x+5)(x-4) & \\le 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x+5)(x-4)=0.$ The roots are $x=-5$ and $x=4,$ and they split the number line into $3$ subintervals $(-\\infty ,-5),$ $(-5,4)$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-5)$ | $(-5,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x+5$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x+5)(x-4)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x+5)(x-4)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x+5)(x-4)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(-5,4).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-5$ and $x=4.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $\\frac{1}{10}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{10}x\\le 2$ for all $x\\in [-5,4].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257345 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a3974794-7f69-529e-88a6-c77f4d353d57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257347}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3974794-7f69-529e-88a6-c77f4d353d57" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257347} :answer-field/id #uuid "47fcc8cc-fc22-5cf8-8870-a191a5c947f2" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257346}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dca028c6-39fc-5c90-b60c-c0a6062705b3" :question/math-academy-id "q-119481" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\operatorname{cos}(-{75}^{∘}).$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $-{75}^{∘}={60}^{∘}-{135}^{∘}.$*\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x={60}^{∘}$ and $y={135}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(-{75}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}({60}^{∘}-{135}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}{60}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{135}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}{60}^{∘}\\operatorname{sin}{135}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257389 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "143766e0-0ef5-5caf-b699-d394d4a707e9" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Products"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4f5d5009-47b4-5e9b-a6bb-e3fcfec21e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257391}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4f5d5009-47b4-5e9b-a6bb-e3fcfec21e83" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257391} :answer-field/id #uuid "ae21b87a-8faf-5500-8005-d41884c80814" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257390}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f30800d-77df-5a61-9a68-2afbf867dda1" :question/math-academy-id "q-119947" :question/problem "Simplify ${(\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))}^{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parenthesis and use the Pythagorean identity:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+{cos}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =[{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+{cos}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})]+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =1+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}))}^{2}=1+2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257392 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9683d9-05b9-5a42-9f81-0170a15dfac1" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Ratios"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45a4cfcd-2df2-5469-bc80-8639d0f34a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257394}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45a4cfcd-2df2-5469-bc80-8639d0f34a61" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257394} :answer-field/id #uuid "aa6bc17e-27f5-5be0-8522-dd8f97b112b0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257393}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "b566fa84-3938-5596-bfbc-9b6e5426b71c" :question/math-academy-id "q-120026" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}{80}^{∘}\\operatorname{tan}{40}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}2x=2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={40}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot {40}^{∘}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘} \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}({80}^{∘}) & =2\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}{80}^{∘}\\operatorname{tan}{40}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}\\cdot \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}-1}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}-1+{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}-(1-{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing  the Pythagorean identity in the form ${cos}^{2}{40}^{∘}=1-{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}-(1-{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} & =\\frac{{sin}^{2}{40}^{∘}-{cos}^{2}{40}^{∘}}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{(\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘})(\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘})}{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{(\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘})}(\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘})}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}+\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}}} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}{40}^{∘}-\\operatorname{cos}{40}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257395 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c898bbf-004c-5905-a0a0-761adefc8bd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Value of a Trigonometric Expression Using the Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "04478505-52f9-5abc-a41b-d38c1d9667ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "04478505-52f9-5abc-a41b-d38c1d9667ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257397} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff46dd56-42d2-5fb6-b021-9f93b364efd6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257396}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "766de5e3-4cf3-5599-8f12-d24d0a317252" :question/math-academy-id "q-120033" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}).$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{8}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}) & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{5\\pi }{8}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{8})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257398 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c898bbf-004c-5905-a0a0-761adefc8bd8" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Value of a Trigonometric Expression Using the Double-Angle Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c946f316-e0f1-528f-b04f-487fc62bc3a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257400}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c946f316-e0f1-528f-b04f-487fc62bc3a1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257400} :answer-field/id #uuid "df3ccf03-d2f1-511e-9dd1-7c82faa09dd6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257399}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d593e11-f74c-57da-a9e4-9637db488d53" :question/math-academy-id "q-120036" :question/problem "Find the exact value of ${(\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})-\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}))}^{2}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we expand the parenthesis and use the Pythagorean identity.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})-\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}))}^{2} & ={sin}^{2}({165}^{∘})-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘})+{cos}^{2}({165}^{∘}) \\\\  & =[{sin}^{2}({165}^{∘})+{cos}^{2}({165}^{∘})]-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}) \\\\  & =1-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x={165}^{∘}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot {165}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}({330}^{∘}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n1-2\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘})=1-(-\\frac{1}{2})=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n{(\\operatorname{sin}({165}^{∘})+\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}))}^{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186257404 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "d820aa9f-7c02-53b5-9b09-c25ee59dbdaf" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula Multiple Times"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6cf796a4-4c3a-534e-b51a-7e3a28c8b17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257406}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6cf796a4-4c3a-534e-b51a-7e3a28c8b17f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257406} :answer-field/id #uuid "703becf2-8ebd-59db-8678-340435930cf0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186257405}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "07ab80ff-a6ba-5457-8088-8c6b6073aa0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-120094" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{16}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})=\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{16})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8}).\n$$\n\nNow, we can rewrite the given expression using the above, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{16})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sec}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, using the double-angle formula once more with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{8}$, we can further simplify the above expression: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{2}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} & =\\frac{4}{2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{8})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258060 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b7b17a61-b225-5493-98b0-58d15217aa31" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations by Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "93a31339-31a3-5548-bf82-70f72dcd1507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258062}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "93a31339-31a3-5548-bf82-70f72dcd1507" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258062} :answer-field/id #uuid "d57d861f-3d8d-57dd-8e05-1e213f99e9da" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258061} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "185d00bb-bfee-58d5-bff4-ce7e519b0b5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258064}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "185d00bb-bfee-58d5-bff4-ce7e519b0b5a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258064} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d0f6b9d-a073-50a3-a515-a83016733c3a" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258063} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cd1e4715-9c75-571a-84a1-fe4ac2f9cee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258066}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cd1e4715-9c75-571a-84a1-fe4ac2f9cee0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258066} :answer-field/id #uuid "006449f7-6d61-52f7-9cf8-f904f2c8bc28" :answer-field/key "root-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258065}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b72a04f0-5d72-598f-8e4e-47af21a1c978" :question/math-academy-id "q-14077" :question/problem "Solve ${x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-3x-21=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{3}+7{x}^{2}-3x-21 & = \\\\ ({x}^{3}+7{x}^{2})-(3x+21) & = \\\\ {x}^{2}(x+7)-3(x+7) & = \\\\ (x+7)\\cdot {x}^{2}-(x+7)\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ (x+7)({x}^{2}-3) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(x+7)({x}^{2}-3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(x+7)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+7 & =0 \\\\ x & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({x}^{2}-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=-7,\\,\\pm \\sqrt{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258070 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6158975-c81e-5621-aa39-3f2dc728f107" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations by Manipulating the Equation and Then Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bf7f6163-5a66-5876-95e6-e339fb1c5461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{\\frac12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258072}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bf7f6163-5a66-5876-95e6-e339fb1c5461" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{\\frac12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258072} :answer-field/id #uuid "cf6675f8-a4aa-5984-8ccc-e76815c33031" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258071} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "863d4373-f978-5bdf-afc1-eb2ef0d0ba7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258074}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "863d4373-f978-5bdf-afc1-eb2ef0d0ba7e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac12}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258074} :answer-field/id #uuid "f8c6f6f4-c82a-55b6-9a6c-3baf693504a6" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258073} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "090c71a3-6149-5cb3-97a9-7eb38432e1dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258076}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "090c71a3-6149-5cb3-97a9-7eb38432e1dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258076} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f8c7ff1-6e87-561c-8ba0-98a20d7cf5f2" :answer-field/key "root-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258075}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "23a22035-6f86-5f86-abdd-41c57b6c7a46" :question/math-academy-id "q-14102" :question/problem "Solve $6{y}^{3}+4=8{y}^{2}+3y.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we subtract $8{y}^{2}$ and $3y$ from both sides to make the right side $0.$ To prepare for factoring by grouping, we make sure to write the resulting cubic in standard form.\n\n$$\n6{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2}-3y+4=0\n$$\n\nNext, we factor by grouping. Notice that when grouping the smaller terms, we factor out the negative sign.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}6{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2}-3y+4 & = \\\\ (6{y}^{3}-8{y}^{2})-(3y-4) & = \\\\ 2{y}^{2}(3y-4)-(3y-4) & = \\\\ (3y-4)\\cdot 2{y}^{2}-(3y-4)\\cdot 1 & = \\\\ (3y-4)(2{y}^{2}-1) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we set each factor equal to $0$ and solve.\n\n- Setting the factor $(3y-4)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3y-4 & =0 \\\\ 3y & =4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{4}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2{y}^{2}-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{y}^{2}-1 & =0 \\\\ 2{y}^{2} & =1 \\\\ {y}^{2} & =\\frac{1}{2} \\\\ y & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $y=\\frac{4}{3},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258077 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "385b1ccd-ea68-5c59-a0e6-1a0bb7937460" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations With Leading Coefficients by Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "17396678-9a2d-5efb-84e0-167a80500811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{\\frac37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258079}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17396678-9a2d-5efb-84e0-167a80500811" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{\\frac37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258079} :answer-field/id #uuid "a1ed5062-5f0b-50a3-a428-6b6b4bc3dc03" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258078} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14c5b409-986b-54df-9960-17c7058f6de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258081}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14c5b409-986b-54df-9960-17c7058f6de6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258081} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d3fe96f-e1fa-5548-9950-4dd6d162540c" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258080} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "debcef87-92f3-5cd2-8265-3e8c4b8197a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258083}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "debcef87-92f3-5cd2-8265-3e8c4b8197a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\sqrt{\\frac37}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258083} :answer-field/id #uuid "0df97483-d5f1-54c1-aecd-03d377b92d8c" :answer-field/key "root-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258082}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a40984bf-4ba0-5fcb-b3fc-f4dcbc8bd041" :question/math-academy-id "q-14108" :question/problem "Solve $14{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}-6x+3=0.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}\n\nValue 3 (least to greatest): {{root-3}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}14{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2}-6x+3 & = \\\\ (14{x}^{3}-7{x}^{2})-(6x-3) & = \\\\ 7{x}^{2}(2x-1)-3(2x-1) & = \\\\ (2x-1)\\cdot 7{x}^{2}-(2x-1)\\cdot 3 & = \\\\ (2x-1)(7{x}^{2}-3) & \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(2x-1)(7{x}^{2}-3)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(2x-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =0 \\\\ 2x & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $(7{x}^{2}-3)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7{x}^{2}-3 & =0 \\\\ 7{x}^{2} & =3 \\\\ {x}^{2} & =\\frac{3}{7} \\\\ x & =\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{7}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solutions are $x=\\frac{1}{2},\\,\\pm \\sqrt{\\frac{3}{7}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258084 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36d4bf37-4818-58ac-9e39-75ce8ceef06a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Less Than or Equal To\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "30eb643b-1b75-5ffb-9cf0-9e7f3f40f301" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258086}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "30eb643b-1b75-5ffb-9cf0-9e7f3f40f301" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\ge 0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258086} :answer-field/id #uuid "d6ef7c0b-8167-5f92-b5f2-d39b028bb38f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258085}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dee958e0-6ef4-559d-9402-576ab9209618" :question/math-academy-id "q-141106" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/b70f78004ad1239cc6d7fb46086f8b961d587e69ff5c3a7414beba60cbcf74a9.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-x(x+6)\\le 0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-x(x+6)\\le 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-x(x+6)\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/ed78f461be7f5627814b8df3a85bedcbafbb1456c2dda01e825b0706d164b685.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -6$ or $x\\ge 0.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258087 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "385b1ccd-ea68-5c59-a0e6-1a0bb7937460" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Cubic Equations With Leading Coefficients by Grouping"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e0adb4eb-4e10-57f3-a3ac-5158e7bc1653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258089}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e0adb4eb-4e10-57f3-a3ac-5158e7bc1653" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258089} :answer-field/id #uuid "214026e5-fe18-58f7-8562-0bab5c37ec87" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258088}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fe52eb5f-7e5b-51d0-b560-47f98d7ed314" :question/math-academy-id "q-14121" :question/problem "Find all the real solutions to the equation $3{z}^{3}-{z}^{2}+27z-9=0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We proceed to factor the left-hand side by grouping:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{z}^{3}-{z}^{2}+27z-9 & = \\\\ (3{z}^{3}-{z}^{2})+(27z-9) & = \\\\ {z}^{2}(3z-1)+9(3z-1) & = \\\\ (3z-1)\\cdot {z}^{2}+(3z-1)\\cdot 9 & = \\\\ (3z-1)({z}^{2}+9) & .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we solve $(3z-1)({z}^{2}+9)=0$ by setting each factor equal to $0.$\n\n- Setting the factor $(3z-1)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3z-1 & =0 \\\\ 3z & =1 \\\\ z & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Setting the factor $({z}^{2}+9)$ equal to $0,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{z}^{2}+9 & =0 \\\\ {z}^{2} & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince a squared real number cannot be negative, the equation ${z}^{2}+9=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore,  $z=\\frac{1}{3}$ is the only real solution."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258093 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5337e4-9289-52fc-ba01-a0e7a8df6285" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cf3e47f4-a46a-599a-b9cb-8d68865b7590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({n}^{2}+36)(n+6)(n-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258095}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf3e47f4-a46a-599a-b9cb-8d68865b7590" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({n}^{2}+36)(n+6)(n-6)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258095} :answer-field/id #uuid "9dfc8025-55f3-55e5-ba54-9cc9e891ef17" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258094}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d866302b-c2d5-51c8-89dc-11e9ed67ca2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-14130" :question/problem "Factor ${n}^{4}-{36}^{2}$ completely.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{n}^{4}-{36}^{2}={({n}^{2})}^{2}-{36}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{({n}^{2})}^{2}-{36}^{2}=({n}^{2}+36)({n}^{2}-36)\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({n}^{2}+36)({n}^{2}-36) & =({n}^{2}+36)({n}^{2}-{6}^{2}) \\\\  & =({n}^{2}+36)(n+6)(n-6)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258102 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b38492a4-3e16-5cd9-ab86-d08e043362ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258104}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b38492a4-3e16-5cd9-ab86-d08e043362ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258104} :answer-field/id #uuid "55521609-be41-59e3-8855-f2ba75ddf06e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258103}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4e30af5f-93af-52ef-810a-d66a6247d423" :question/math-academy-id "q-141380" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot 0-\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258284 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5dff5b7c-544a-5fda-a175-51ce4723a135" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Greater Than or Equal To\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ab9002a-da67-5693-8558-10c77f908601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\le x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258286}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2ab9002a-da67-5693-8558-10c77f908601" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\le x\\le 4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258286} :answer-field/id #uuid "2aa6335f-3929-5d9d-8d32-c909af10b54e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258285}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f22377a6-66a6-5350-ac0e-892b1407ed25" :question/math-academy-id "q-143365" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/469d229fba26a06ae27dd6014230124cc4b379c72d5cc8aa8b6ab4d026903eeb.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-8+6x-{x}^{2}\\ge 0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-8+6x-{x}^{2}\\ge 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-8+6x-{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis *above* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/2929d99093fa5c658059ce0470e5d1e6fa293dcad3a8a5eff1aa83fc05d0a4c3.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $2\\le x\\le 4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258325 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc419917-fe34-5bf9-8bf0-1b022fce11ff" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Less Than\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "99e84602-1398-5b4d-904a-8318edc97f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258327}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "99e84602-1398-5b4d-904a-8318edc97f15" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x>1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258327} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb626680-57c4-56f3-96de-36f87f97430c" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258326}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "6488e91c-d0d1-5b12-999b-f3e91536690e" :question/math-academy-id "q-144415" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/89a11e09dcb47c6f445e4f7921e8d4670453b10b67be3921d5723728ee154381.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $3-2x-{x}^{2}<0.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n3-2x-{x}^{2}<0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola  \n\n$$\ny=3-2x-{x}^{2}\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/a73a7db40f0469ff37c14b0bf52a35f28b08ebf8224ab5b86c97f72d5d12aae1.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-3$ or    $x>1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258713 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb53971d-15be-579e-86f5-4fc8d0c10d6d" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Arithmetic Series Using Sigma Notation when All Terms are Shown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f799ae04-d491-5be0-b200-09d1d66f5a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(9n-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258715}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f799ae04-d491-5be0-b200-09d1d66f5a2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(9n-8)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258715} :answer-field/id #uuid "080aea64-8587-5a83-80aa-5adba4d0f0e8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258714}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "0439465f-c44b-5ef9-840d-c708559f8d6e" :question/math-academy-id "q-17271" :question/problem "The arithmetic series $1+10+19+28+37+46$ can be written in sigma notation as\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The first term is ${a}_{1}=1$ and the common difference is: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =10-1 \\\\  & =9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =1+(n-1)(9) \\\\  & =1+9n-9 \\\\  & =9n-8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $6$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=6.$ So the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(9n-8)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186258934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "765ef63a-e3f0-503b-876d-3b4776b2cafe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Approximate Solutions to Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e388c577-7061-527f-99d6-695d6ff9b4ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.527" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258936}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e388c577-7061-527f-99d6-695d6ff9b4ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-0.527" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258936} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb92bf39-5411-5859-9598-c76250d50b5f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186258935}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dce75f1f-4e44-5de1-9b3a-5c377c4f3af9" :question/math-academy-id "q-228899" :question/problem "If ${e}^{2x+3}=7,$ then $x\\approx ${{field-1}}, rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+3} & =7 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{2x+3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(7) \\\\ 2x+3 & =\\operatorname{ln}(7) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}(7)-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}(7)-3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx\\approx -0.527\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259098 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "765ef63a-e3f0-503b-876d-3b4776b2cafe" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Approximate Solutions to Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5447b780-0ab4-56b4-8a53-e99d01a5e05f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259100}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5447b780-0ab4-56b4-8a53-e99d01a5e05f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.024" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259100} :answer-field/id #uuid "ad7f6d1f-e7da-5ee4-802b-8191318a5090" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259099}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "d4fded30-6fdc-57c4-9ae4-833f7de89572" :question/math-academy-id "q-263084" :question/problem "If ${e}^{1-4x}=\\sqrt[3]{15},$ then $x\\approx ${{field-1}}, rounded to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we take $ln$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{1-4x} & =\\sqrt[3]{15} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{1-4x}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(\\sqrt[3]{15}) \\\\ 1-4x & =\\operatorname{ln}({15}^{1/3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we apply the power rule to the right-hand side and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1-4x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(15) \\\\ -4x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{ln}(15)-1 \\\\ x & =-\\frac{1}{12}\\operatorname{ln}(15)+\\frac{1}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nx\\approx 0.024\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259118 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "bd612e5f-cc72-5df4-94da-e290473ed0a4" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of a Geometric Series Given With a Zero Starting Index"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c6eb5520-8d26-5f65-8501-73db7e4c1c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{312}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259120}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c6eb5520-8d26-5f65-8501-73db7e4c1c5d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{312}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259120} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b0e809c-6844-5731-a64b-72f8fbdcd8bf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259119}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "67a65dcf-aa63-52fc-b7e4-2c741bfe2157" :question/math-academy-id "q-269902" :question/problem "Evaluate the following geometric series:\n\n${\\sum }_{n=0}^{3}2{(5)}^{n-1}=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Since the given series is in the form $\\sum a{r}^{n-1},$ it is a geometric series. Notice that the index starts from $n=0$ instead of $n=1.$\n\nTo determine the sum, we use the formula for the sum ${S}_{N}$ of a geometric series.\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}={a}_{0}\\frac{(1-{r}^{N})}{1-r}\n$$\n\nIn this case, $r=5.$ Also, note that $N=4,$ since there are $4$ terms in total.\n\nThe formula for the $n$th term of the series is\n\n$$\n{a}_{n}=2{(5)}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nand so the first term ${a}_{0}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{0} & =2{(5)}^{0-1} \\\\  & =2{(5)}^{-1} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{5} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we substitute ${a}_{0}=\\frac{2}{5},r=5$ and $N=4$ into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{4} & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{4})}{(1-5)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}\\cdot \\frac{(1-{5}^{4})}{(-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1248}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{312}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259121 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eec195c-6549-5de2-b3f0-0417e5f518e7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Greater Than\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36328637-9412-587e-a918-9062d6aeaf47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259123}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36328637-9412-587e-a918-9062d6aeaf47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259123} :answer-field/id #uuid "198d6e70-00b9-5595-b743-7bae5608c115" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259122} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "55ee629e-2f4f-56da-923a-ed5c8ceb82f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259125}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "55ee629e-2f4f-56da-923a-ed5c8ceb82f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259125} :answer-field/id #uuid "1f7cf7a2-a5f0-518e-a840-0d6a584a7028" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259124} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba5ac51f-011f-553c-9e36-c7254e9e2989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259127}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba5ac51f-011f-553c-9e36-c7254e9e2989" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259127} :answer-field/id #uuid "9046ca6b-4916-52c7-b0a9-671b37cefacd" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259126}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "72b318f0-d031-5113-b6de-a73c45f90d0b" :question/math-academy-id "q-270581" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/dab886380436be716e80864ac6f58503052c7962995ff65e7f6b0bc1a7a6e22e.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $(x-4)(x+2)>0.$\n\nThe solution to the inequality is $x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nor $x>$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n(x-4)(x+2)>0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola\n\n$$\ny=(x-4)(x+2)\n$$\n\n \nis *above* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/47aafc516c10a98a8302340bea8b0cc2239dad13cabfbe548e6e2cdadea2def6.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-2$ or $x>4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259139 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6a58962-cb06-519a-8c64-0dfa3b9c14d7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "49d8700b-9994-5907-ba2e-c4f20fa828df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259141}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "49d8700b-9994-5907-ba2e-c4f20fa828df" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\operatorname{sin}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259141} :answer-field/id #uuid "0412563f-3360-572e-b136-33ab483bc5e4" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259140}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f9b6607b-b7a2-5915-bcf5-69205d886d0d" :question/math-academy-id "q-274499" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}(x-{270}^{∘})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}x$ or $\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}(x-{270}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v+\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={270}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x-{270}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}{270}^{∘}+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}{270}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot 0+\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259194 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d5d60ca-ce6e-5818-80f6-5e1f00a4ea32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259196}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2d5d60ca-ce6e-5818-80f6-5e1f00a4ea32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259196} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec5da6d3-c4f9-5015-b5e8-9cbbee98e21c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259195} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "983e13cd-944c-5cec-a2ca-63d865a191e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259198}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "983e13cd-944c-5cec-a2ca-63d865a191e0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259198} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7d70ad1-2f32-53da-941b-6509c9c8c13c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259197}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "af193db4-aced-5259-96b5-34c474664303" :question/math-academy-id "q-278249" :question/problem "In **ascending order**, the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{3}-3{x}^{2}}$ are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}$\\,$ and $\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+2}{{x}^{2}(x-3)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n{x}^{2}(x-3)=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=0$ and $x=3.$ \n\nTherefore, in ascending order, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=0$ and $x=3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259199 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b147d074-50c4-579f-b791-a85cf421ccbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b147d074-50c4-579f-b791-a85cf421ccbe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259201} :answer-field/id #uuid "0f43ee4a-f94f-5cde-ad17-a3f76d1effde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259200} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e9142388-b025-5efb-95cb-753d76095add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e9142388-b025-5efb-95cb-753d76095add" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259203} :answer-field/id #uuid "d68d6d82-a6c8-5f79-977b-16b32614d59a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259202} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7e7529f6-6980-56a6-af05-5ca35dd024d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7e7529f6-6980-56a6-af05-5ca35dd024d3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259205} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9c70dbb-3a1d-59ff-9170-ea4bcf75b936" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259204}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "afea9ca1-a406-5faa-9e2a-dda897028d2b" :question/math-academy-id "q-278308" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-6x+8>0?$\n\n$x\\in (-\\infty ,$\n{{field-1}}\n$)\\cup ($\n{{field-2}}\n$,$\n{{field-3}}\n$)$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-6x+8 & >0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-4) & >0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x-2)(x-4)=0.$ The roots are $x=2$ and $x=4,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,2),$ $(2,4),$ and $(4,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,4)$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-4$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x-2)(x-4)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x-2)(x-4)>0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-2)(x-4)$ is positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(4,\\infty ).$ \n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-6x+8>0$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,2)\\cup (4,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a186b166-928c-5bad-a996-3bdf6af419f5" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "629dbfeb-8dc3-5f39-8e05-f05c351569fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259229}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "629dbfeb-8dc3-5f39-8e05-f05c351569fa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\infty " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259229} :answer-field/id #uuid "5d636d3b-e96b-5e1c-b78b-028540cfddae" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259228} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8261317c-0bee-507f-b6dc-f94b7ba3293e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259231}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8261317c-0bee-507f-b6dc-f94b7ba3293e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259231} :answer-field/id #uuid "b33f94b9-983c-5726-8b8f-69adf3effaa7" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259230} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "73d4794d-e6b6-50d2-9a4c-312c4a547b2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259233}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "73d4794d-e6b6-50d2-9a4c-312c4a547b2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259233} :answer-field/id #uuid "be4de963-e6bd-5c21-be4e-be63b64e47c1" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259232}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "baa13e69-8ea6-5662-aba0-a6423851ad65" :question/math-academy-id "q-278408" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}-7x+10\\ge 0?$\n\n$x\\in ($\n{{field-1}}\n$,$\n{{field-2}}\n$]\\cup [$\n{{field-3}}\n$,\\,\\infty )$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}-7x+10 & \\ge 0 \\\\ (x-2)(x-5) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $(x-2)(x-5)=0.$ The roots are $x=2$ and $x=5,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,2),$ $(2,5),$ and $(5,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,2)$ | $(2,5)$ | $(5,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-5$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $(x-2)(x-5)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $(x-2)(x-5)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $(x-2)(x-5)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,2)$ and $(5,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=2$ and $x=5.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}-7x+10\\ge 0$ for all \n\n$$\nx\\in (-\\infty ,2]\\cup [5,\\infty ).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259234 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b23c4da-7a8d-5076-a4e9-8bcde40bc088" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Table of Signs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e515fca7-d384-5d66-a1d6-049e32ecf2f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259236} {:answer/id #uuid "8af5ce15-e8cf-5aa3-bd2b-5ff924815fa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259237}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8af5ce15-e8cf-5aa3-bd2b-5ff924815fa3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259237} :answer-field/id #uuid "190f715e-8178-501c-a5fe-f56a5c43a5be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259235} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "50ce3c0f-09e2-5ee9-9d78-56fb4988d116" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259239} {:answer/id #uuid "46e00440-7bea-5ee9-91b6-1b33585d2d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259240}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "46e00440-7bea-5ee9-91b6-1b33585d2d22" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259240} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb50c58a-e653-5d5d-a2be-7821a4a7e44c" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259238} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3a2ea4cf-4e02-5d81-9a2c-e35e147e2d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259242} {:answer/id #uuid "edf49be4-466c-5d14-8b4a-d8dd032a788a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259243}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3a2ea4cf-4e02-5d81-9a2c-e35e147e2d11" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259242} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee27fcfc-c49a-5f2d-ac9b-76d82cff94ff" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259241}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ed24486a-03ba-53f0-a903-d0d663446f22" :question/math-academy-id "q-278465" :question/problem "Choose the appropriate signs for the table shown below.\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,15)$ | $(15,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-15$ | {{field-1}} | {{field-2}} | {{field-3}} |" :question/worked-solution "- On the interval $(-\\infty ,0),$ we can choose a test point ${x}_{1}=-1.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-15 & =-1-15=(-),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,15)$ | $(15,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-15$ | $-$ |  |  |\n\n- On the interval $(0,15),$ we can choose a test point ${x}_{1}=10.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-15 & =10-15=(-),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,15)$ | $(15,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-15$ | $-$ | $-$ |  |\n\n- On the interval $(15,\\infty ),$ we can choose a test point ${x}_{1}=100.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-15 & =100-15=(+),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,15)$ | $(15,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-15$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259244 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4b23c4da-7a8d-5076-a4e9-8bcde40bc088" :knowledge-point/title "Completing a Table of Signs"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b62c04b0-6812-530d-9197-4668529b28af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259246} {:answer/id #uuid "120d53c6-a3cc-5667-9eff-f384ec59f0d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259247}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "120d53c6-a3cc-5667-9eff-f384ec59f0d7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259247} :answer-field/id #uuid "db822a7c-c5af-5c9e-9c80-a0498c62bbec" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259245} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c5a20c9-ecca-5e41-a63a-f46edb4f3e0d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259249} {:answer/id #uuid "81895b6d-ca1e-5d8a-883e-481679c1d5f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259250}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81895b6d-ca1e-5d8a-883e-481679c1d5f2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259250} :answer-field/id #uuid "531c01f1-1bd3-5f91-bd15-26bac604208a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259248} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36cdcc63-54f6-5ea7-9963-7b4a2ef25625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259252} {:answer/id #uuid "aa2bf3e3-5f95-5505-858e-1164eaee5d70" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259253}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36cdcc63-54f6-5ea7-9963-7b4a2ef25625" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "+" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259252} :answer-field/id #uuid "75a0e999-d0c5-5624-a4cf-f4582a83b873" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259251}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "03004bfd-df6a-59c0-9192-a6eab9a85f53" :question/math-academy-id "q-278488" :question/problem "What are the missing entries in the sign table below?\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-12)$ | $(-12,11)$ | $(11,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-11$ | {{field-1}} | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+12$ | {{field-2}} | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $(x-11)(x+12)$ | {{field-3}} | $-$ | $+$ |" :question/worked-solution "On the interval $(-\\infty ,-12),$ we can choose a test point ${x}_{1}=-20.$ Then we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-11 & =-20-11=(-), \\\\ x+12 & =-20+12=(-), \\\\ (x-11)(x+12) & =(-)\\times (-)=(+),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand we fill in the table as follows:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-12)$ | $(-12,11)$ | $(11,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x-11$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+12$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $(x-11)(x+12)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259254 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7cf57b73-5ad2-5eab-bf6d-fb4d095f013d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259256}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7cf57b73-5ad2-5eab-bf6d-fb4d095f013d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259256} :answer-field/id #uuid "06303f5e-9a77-5a76-93dc-2cd6a3f05bfe" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259255}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0df3445d-c4fe-56f7-8f72-bac44d3d2fd6" :question/math-academy-id "q-278536" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{x}^{2}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}}{\\cancel{{x}^{2}}(x-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{x-2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-2=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=2.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259257 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "53b26ee4-53af-5660-a9e6-8e5cca8b3e1d" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes With Common Factors in the Numerator and Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3b58eef-9b07-5e62-8dfd-06cdd9792dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259259}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3b58eef-9b07-5e62-8dfd-06cdd9792dd0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259259} :answer-field/id #uuid "e3d0c0c1-1bcd-51f6-8e4a-70dcf5913bcc" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259258}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "8e85e93c-3cfa-5608-97e9-59cee6be71eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-278543" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{2}+x-2}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator and canceling any common factors, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-x-6}{{x}^{2}+x-2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)(x+2)}{(x-1)(x+2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-3)\\cancel{(x+2)}}{(x-1)\\cancel{(x+2)}} \\\\  & =\\frac{x-3}{x-1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\nx-1=0\n$$\n\nThis gives the solution $x=1.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259260 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6bca57a-bbd7-5135-b4d1-5394e2880a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259262}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6bca57a-bbd7-5135-b4d1-5394e2880a4c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259262} :answer-field/id #uuid "2bbf306f-0153-5b3f-845e-2e452538f0cf" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259261}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5f2dc1ae-4892-50b9-981f-808f974086b6" :question/math-academy-id "q-278551" :question/problem "The vertical asymptote of $f(x)=\\frac{4}{2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-1}$ is $x=${{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{4}{2{x}^{3}-2{x}^{2}+x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{2{x}^{2}(x-1)+(x-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4}{(2{x}^{2}+1)(x-1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\n(2{x}^{2}+1)(x-1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x-1=0$ gives the solution $x=1.$\n\n- The equation $2{x}^{2}+1=0$ has no real solutions.\n\nTherefore, the vertical asymptote is $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259603 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "36d4bf37-4818-58ac-9e39-75ce8ceef06a" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Less Than or Equal To\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9fe08400-ef5a-5e19-86dd-e40b5f494f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259605}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9fe08400-ef5a-5e19-86dd-e40b5f494f94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259605} :answer-field/id #uuid "c50dbe77-d135-57d0-9cb9-7fb3abe07433" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259604} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d5ddc88e-e53a-5e20-bf3a-ef5c14a3bacd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259607}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d5ddc88e-e53a-5e20-bf3a-ef5c14a3bacd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\ge " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259607} :answer-field/id #uuid "7dba7302-3b20-5a28-be96-eec1f27df504" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259606} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6fdc06ea-d729-5a48-a5df-ecd6a0c913ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259609}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6fdc06ea-d729-5a48-a5df-ecd6a0c913ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259609} :answer-field/id #uuid "29b8d5e2-d38b-571f-847a-19e80ad52aee" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259608}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1abfe16-544e-536a-be8b-9d5556c70b80" :question/math-academy-id "q-326144" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/9e2575f2e20fb3146a92768eeedb32bed1bdb1ddb69f68b55c925a8f9898b695.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-(x-5)(x+3)\\le 0.$\n\nThe solution to the inequality is $x\\le $\n{{field-1}}\nor $x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-(x-5)(x+3)\\le 0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than or equal to*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola \n\n$$\ny=-(x-5)(x+3)\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis or *touches* this axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/68d77d138842f95fbc055d5f8a0adda2bb7b04f8a133ce02d4813a54265e883b.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x\\le -3$ or $x\\ge 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259629 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc419917-fe34-5bf9-8bf0-1b022fce11ff" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Less Than\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f0e18a3e-e437-5238-81ec-648bc6251417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259631}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f0e18a3e-e437-5238-81ec-648bc6251417" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259631} :answer-field/id #uuid "8337e4b4-8dc2-5dac-b1a4-957add5cd55d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259630} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c14e51f8-54c9-5150-b5e6-e068eb1aef2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c14e51f8-54c9-5150-b5e6-e068eb1aef2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259633} :answer-field/id #uuid "64529d7f-ca5b-5e31-aee6-dc1acb257443" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259632} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "32bdd285-de23-5070-ba8c-7fdd6f29175b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259635}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "32bdd285-de23-5070-ba8c-7fdd6f29175b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259635} :answer-field/id #uuid "287ff432-c06b-535e-b2f8-726a3d52b5e7" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259634}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d4e62bc6-3f98-510b-80a4-0b7f3a3e5668" :question/math-academy-id "q-327473" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/0279244ec15b12209fa0a18b46bb9a52622878221c9d424b082f73feefae109d.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-(x-4)(x+1)<0.$\n\nThe solution to the inequality is \n$x$\n{{field-1}}{{field-2}}\nor $x>$\n{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-(x-4)(x+1)<0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*less than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola\n\n$$\ny=-(x-4)(x+1)\n$$\n\nis *below* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/3153ee62691b0000cbf249553c93a7304c3a2908d2ea7810874c5ed37e68a412.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $x<-1$ or $x>4.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259636 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "3eec195c-6549-5de2-b3f0-0417e5f518e7" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a \"Greater Than\" Inequality Given the Graph of the Corresponding Parabola"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3288736e-6f5c-50d6-8b7b-ebc88d608e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259638}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3288736e-6f5c-50d6-8b7b-ebc88d608e1c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259638} :answer-field/id #uuid "7da33b69-14fe-594b-8b1b-b35ccf76db47" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259637} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "94d3b5f0-2cb7-51f8-bc28-ee01d1025e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "94d3b5f0-2cb7-51f8-bc28-ee01d1025e47" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "<" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259640} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9b8e9a1-90e8-5cbf-aa55-0193e94c694a" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259639} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f7baffe6-f715-53fb-ba6e-3e5bd328657a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259642}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7baffe6-f715-53fb-ba6e-3e5bd328657a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259642} :answer-field/id #uuid "d0af7488-dde8-5f4d-85b7-2b6201e188bd" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259641}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "dd2edf84-b845-52b7-b563-a7923b04f23a" :question/math-academy-id "q-327529" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/55b40acad6aed639934c751bbc0b5dadcf7a6a1169931d19e9bec9de60401f81.png)\n\nGiven the graph above, solve the inequality $-{x}^{2}+4x+5>0.$\n\nThe solution to the inequality is\n{{field-1}}\n$<x$\n{{field-2}}{{field-3}}." :question/worked-solution "Notice that our inequality\n\n$$\n-{x}^{2}+4x+5>0,\n$$\n\nis a \"*greater than*\" inequality. Therefore, we need to find the values of $x$ for which the parabola\n\n$$\ny=-{x}^{2}+4x+5\n$$\n\n \nis *above* the $x$-axis. We mark these values of $x$ in blue in the diagram below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/history-question-import-2026-10-04/import-ready/assets/373430993d1b1d46a92b44ed897c5e4570b6a9c9072e5b9a4af2b854b7801c81.png)\n\nSo, the solution to the inequality is $-1<x<5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259719 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4c40a2ff-aaab-5f5f-84a6-6f23e65956a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259721}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4c40a2ff-aaab-5f5f-84a6-6f23e65956a5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259721} :answer-field/id #uuid "df6b3482-1bd2-53a3-bb18-5c91166f75ff" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259720}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6ba27561-086b-5243-b9dc-bb922689b561" :question/math-academy-id "q-332487" :question/problem "Using the fact that $\\frac{11\\pi }{12}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{6},$\ncalculate the **exact** value of $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{11\\pi }{12}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{11\\pi }{12})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{11\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})-\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259722 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e10e5597-fec0-529e-b36f-156c634cadd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259724}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e10e5597-fec0-529e-b36f-156c634cadd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259724} :answer-field/id #uuid "6081c00e-fe42-5588-96f8-ae69b2f862f5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259723}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4aec515d-e991-53f9-902d-f194d5baa993" :question/math-academy-id "q-332488" :question/problem "Using the fact that $\\frac{17\\pi }{12}=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$\ncalculate the **exact** value of $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{17\\pi }{12}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{17\\pi }{12})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{4}$ and $y=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{17\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4}+\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})-\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}-\\sqrt{6}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259725 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "584f48f4-d27c-5487-ba3d-f4d6f8bd5cb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259727}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "584f48f4-d27c-5487-ba3d-f4d6f8bd5cb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259727} :answer-field/id #uuid "efc71595-d10d-5843-97da-a8f4dd720b97" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259726}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ef62c9f9-a095-50cb-9ce1-245f17a582f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-332492" :question/problem "Using the fact that ${75}^{∘}={45}^{∘}+{30}^{∘},$\ncalculate the **exact** value of $\\operatorname{cos}({75}^{∘}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}({75}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x={45}^{∘}$ and $y={30}^{∘}$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}({75}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}({45}^{∘}+{30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}({45}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({30}^{∘})-\\operatorname{sin}({45}^{∘})\\operatorname{sin}({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{6}-\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259728 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fabff5cf-2fca-5042-ba54-3685ecd36b7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259730}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fabff5cf-2fca-5042-ba54-3685ecd36b7b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259730} :answer-field/id #uuid "5fbc4e67-494b-534c-b603-48fdc7e2bbb1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259729}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "43a1b2b0-4d34-5f4d-8a4e-7b0cc94830d7" :question/math-academy-id "q-332497" :question/problem "Using the fact that $-{165}^{∘}={60}^{∘}-{225}^{∘},$\ncalculate the **exact** value of $\\operatorname{cos}(-{165}^{∘}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}(-{165}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x={60}^{∘}$ and $y={225}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(-{165}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}({60}^{∘}-{225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}({60}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({225}^{∘})+\\operatorname{sin}({60}^{∘})\\operatorname{sin}({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259731 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51848a55-9dcd-5809-8d1d-b784e728b2d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259733}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "51848a55-9dcd-5809-8d1d-b784e728b2d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259733} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d6cd35c-99df-53bf-a223-b226bd97ad44" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259732}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "c17df135-ee84-5f51-838d-783803b3c840" :question/math-academy-id "q-332500" :question/problem "Using the fact that ${120}^{∘}={150}^{∘}-{30}^{∘},$\ncalculate the **exact** value of $\\operatorname{cos}({120}^{∘}).$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}({120}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x={150}^{∘}$ and $y={30}^{∘},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}({120}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}({150}^{∘}-{30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}({150}^{∘})\\operatorname{cos}({30}^{∘})+\\operatorname{sin}({150}^{∘})\\operatorname{sin}({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}+\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{3}{4}+\\frac{1}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186259734 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c6a58962-cb06-519a-8c64-0dfa3b9c14d7" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Degrees Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d636191-15fc-5c2a-b9c9-8f4943ad9652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259736}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4d636191-15fc-5c2a-b9c9-8f4943ad9652" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259736} :answer-field/id #uuid "063dfd94-2bbe-514c-a093-9dceaa47e537" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259735} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ba279fd-6d34-5a0a-a655-9a8a46435c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259738}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0ba279fd-6d34-5a0a-a655-9a8a46435c31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259738} :answer-field/id #uuid "7aa0ccc9-583d-58a8-865d-696a168eb38e" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186259737}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b25c8bbb-7711-5aeb-aaeb-86aea98f9435" :question/math-academy-id "q-332509" :question/problem "Express $\\operatorname{cos}(x+{330}^{∘})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}x$ and $\\operatorname{cos}x.$\n\n$\\operatorname{cos}(x+{330}^{∘})=$\n{{field-1}}$\\operatorname{sin}x\\,+$\n{{field-2}}$\\operatorname{cos}x$" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u+v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v-\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v={330}^{∘}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x+{330}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}{330}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}{330}^{∘} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\operatorname{sin}x+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\,\\operatorname{cos}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260220 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9bfd9e9-603e-50ae-8cd1-be79b88aa087" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial With Leading Coefficients as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8facd36b-4ffd-5122-899f-ee1d928a9890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260222}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8facd36b-4ffd-5122-899f-ee1d928a9890" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260222} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f870397-1f55-53b8-84e2-4c2fa430d34e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260221}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a639602e-39c5-51b8-b4e5-2591b688f7fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-37702" :question/problem "Factor the expression $\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{2}-24.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that ${x}^{4}$ has a factor of $\\frac{3}{2}.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $\\frac{3}{2}$ in our expression, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3{x}^{4}}{2}-24=\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot {x}^{4}-\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot 16=\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{4}-16)\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{4}-16)=\\frac{3}{2}({({x}^{2})}^{2}-{4}^{2})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}({({x}^{2})}^{2}-{4}^{2})=\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+4)({x}^{2}-4)\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+4)({x}^{2}-4) & =\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+4)({x}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260261 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "60c99086-75e7-5d91-b425-4e1b6f582bcc" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Vertical Asymptotes of a Rational Function"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ba9ce503-e19f-5913-9d26-006b9308551f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260263}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ba9ce503-e19f-5913-9d26-006b9308551f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac32" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260263} :answer-field/id #uuid "771e6309-23e4-5a36-ae53-d5409300995e" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260262} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b4427b7-43fd-59ae-906a-c1bb5d224a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260265}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b4427b7-43fd-59ae-906a-c1bb5d224a31" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260265} :answer-field/id #uuid "6cfa389f-de85-58d6-9df0-88cfe98f06cb" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260264}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "81b756f0-9b92-5a8e-a082-00d2ced4e8ae" :question/math-academy-id "q-39790" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{2{x}^{2}+x-3}.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x.$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\nf(x)=\\frac{x+1}{(2x+3)(x-1)}.\n$$\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2x+3)(x-1) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the two solutions $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1.$ Therefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=-\\frac{3}{2}$ and $x=1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260294 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f94b20f-9190-5851-ba54-e7687a223a41" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series Using Sigma Notation With Simplifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "75f74b92-bc0d-519a-88d4-52914aa59956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-2){4}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260296}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "75f74b92-bc0d-519a-88d4-52914aa59956" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-2){4}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260296} :answer-field/id #uuid "d8f73d13-4eeb-55c8-bb70-ee709dd0dc67" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260295}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "fcaed1ea-a740-59c1-9f0c-86cba46523b0" :question/math-academy-id "q-40003" :question/problem "The first term of a geometric sequence is $-8$ and the common ratio is $4.$ Written in sigma notation, what is the sum of the first $30$ terms?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we must express the series in the form:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=-8,$ $r=4$ and $N=30.$\n\nExpressing the geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{S}_{30}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-8){4}^{n-1}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify the above using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-8){4}^{n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-2)\\cdot 4\\cdot {4}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-2)\\cdot {4}^{1+n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{30}(-2){4}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a96557a2-bb28-53a3-b5cd-b8bb2a476c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a96557a2-bb28-53a3-b5cd-b8bb2a476c30" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260373} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d45fed3-648d-5387-b42d-eb8574d012d9" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260372}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1c73da58-2595-51a8-a8de-bdfae4bbae89" :question/math-academy-id "q-43381" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\operatorname{cos}{165}^{∘}.$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that ${165}^{∘}={120}^{∘}+{45}^{∘}.$*\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the sum formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x+y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y-\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the sum formula for cosine with $x={120}^{∘}$ and $y={45}^{∘}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}({165}^{∘}) & =\\operatorname{cos}({120}^{∘}+{45}^{∘}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}{120}^{∘}\\operatorname{cos}{45}^{∘}-\\operatorname{sin}{120}^{∘}\\operatorname{sin}{45}^{∘} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260374 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "831f9aa0-099e-5e0c-8a9d-c4828b629f64" :knowledge-point/title "Simplifying Expressions in Radians Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "96b8b029-c8c4-5881-a673-713bfc764f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x-\\operatorname{cos}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260376}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "96b8b029-c8c4-5881-a673-713bfc764f6e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x-\\operatorname{cos}x)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260376} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e159b75-b237-56c5-b8c2-1fa3a976cc03" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260375}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "3e55af46-7aa0-5eeb-8a65-43726ccf1418" :question/math-academy-id "q-43384" :question/problem "$\\operatorname{cos}(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(u-v)=\\operatorname{cos}u\\operatorname{cos}v+\\operatorname{sin}u\\operatorname{sin}v\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $u=x$ and $v=\\frac{2\\pi }{3},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(x-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3})+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}x\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+\\operatorname{sin}x\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\sqrt{3}\\operatorname{sin}x-\\operatorname{cos}x).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260377 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "143766e0-0ef5-5caf-b699-d394d4a707e9" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Products"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39ce3618-ce33-5fc6-809b-e21af8ad4860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260379}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39ce3618-ce33-5fc6-809b-e21af8ad4860" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260379} :answer-field/id #uuid "8000b167-01ef-591c-8cee-ea82edff7347" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260378}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5b13a054-dc23-5be0-bdef-7ff3c961a76d" :question/math-academy-id "q-43462" :question/problem "Express $6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})$ as a single trigonometric ratio.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x=\\operatorname{sin}2x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{3\\pi }{10}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10}) & =\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{3\\pi }{10})=\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n6\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})=3\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{3\\pi }{10})=3\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{3\\pi }{5}).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260453 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a6460636-9908-59dc-9e66-447743541a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260455}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a6460636-9908-59dc-9e66-447743541a94" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac12" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260455} :answer-field/id #uuid "46d1f1c9-b7e1-599a-82e1-4f2e81294d21" :answer-field/key "root-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260454} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "66d0bdef-2464-576f-9685-817bdc8a8870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260457}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "66d0bdef-2464-576f-9685-817bdc8a8870" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260457} :answer-field/id #uuid "9d831575-8a4a-5fa8-9723-55677181c32e" :answer-field/key "root-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260456}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "119a8c59-2e06-5773-a076-fad28dc30f8e" :question/math-academy-id "q-4698" :question/problem "Determine the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{8{x}^{4}+x}.$\n\nValue 1 (least to greatest): {{root-1}}\n\nValue 2 (least to greatest): {{root-2}}" :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\nFactoring the numerator and denominator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\frac{{x}^{2}-1}{8{x}^{4}+x} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x(8{x}^{3}+1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x[(2x)^{3}+{1}^{3}]} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)(x-1)}{x(2x+1)(4{x}^{2}-2x+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that we used the sum of cubes formula to factor the denominator.\n\nThere are no common factors in the numerator and denominator, so we set the denominator equal to zero. This gives\n\n$$\nx(2x+1)(4{x}^{2}-2x+1)=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve the above equation using the zero product property:\n\n- Solving $x=0$ gives the solution $x=0.$\n\n- Solving $2x+1=0$ gives the solution $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\n- The equation $4{x}^{2}-2x+1=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-4(4)(1) \\\\  & =4-16 \\\\  & =-12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equations of the vertical asymptotes are $x=0$ and $x=-\\frac{1}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7df31c9-b27a-5ce2-93c8-178ba96da000" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2781a87c-c459-54dd-ba3e-a5af81917eb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "640" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260562}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2781a87c-c459-54dd-ba3e-a5af81917eb6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "640" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260562} :answer-field/id #uuid "88dc04f0-ba52-595f-b804-826cb8ee5d73" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260561}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "9cc8a79a-7802-592e-80ef-d176d2e37ddd" :question/math-academy-id "q-48608" :question/problem "The birth rate of a krill population is modeled by $B(t)=5\\cdot {2}^{0.7t}$ thousands of tons per year, where $t$ is the time, in years, since $1998.$ What was the birth rate, in thousands of tons per year, for the krill in $2008?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $B(t)$ models the birth rate for the krill, in thousands of tons, $t$ years since $1998.$ Since we want the birth rate in $2008$, then $t=10$ years.\n\nThus, the birth rate for the krill in $2008$ was\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B(10) & =5\\cdot {2}^{0.7(10)} \\\\  & =5\\cdot {2}^{7} \\\\  & =640.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the birth rate for the krill in $2008$ was $640$ thousand tons per year."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260694 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4c5337e4-9289-52fc-ba01-a0e7a8df6285" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "7920418e-af3c-5c66-b094-87b8928dd98c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({t}^{2}+4)(t+2)(t-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260696}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7920418e-af3c-5c66-b094-87b8928dd98c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "({t}^{2}+4)(t+2)(t-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260696} :answer-field/id #uuid "26cb8504-091f-5e69-84cd-d1b1e36e3f99" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260695}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "706f1b39-71a2-517a-b05a-ddde27e028de" :question/math-academy-id "q-5156" :question/problem "Factor ${t}^{4}-16.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that our expression can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n{t}^{4}-16={({t}^{2})}^{2}-{4}^{2}\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n{({t}^{2})}^{2}-{4}^{2}=({t}^{2}+4)({t}^{2}-4)\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}({t}^{2}+4)({t}^{2}-4) & =({t}^{2}+4)({t}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+4)(t+2)(t-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9bfd9e9-603e-50ae-8cd1-be79b88aa087" :knowledge-point/title "Factoring a Polynomial With Leading Coefficients as a Difference of Squares"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "be9da5a2-0a9d-56da-b319-c3162727f00a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260702}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "be9da5a2-0a9d-56da-b319-c3162727f00a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)(x-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260702} :answer-field/id #uuid "ef137a8f-8a07-57cd-9a28-96dd910518f8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260701}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5a9dd0e6-6456-5413-b50a-4bf96b104572" :question/math-academy-id "q-5165" :question/problem "Factor $3{x}^{4}-48.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The difference of squares formula is given by\n\n$$\n{a}^{2}-{b}^{2}=(a+b)(a-b)\n$$\n\nNotice that all of the terms in our expression have a common factor of $3.$ To simplify the computation, we can factor out the $3,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n3{x}^{4}-48=3\\cdot {x}^{4}-3\\cdot 16=3({x}^{4}-16)\n$$\n\nThe expression in parentheses can be written as a difference of squares:\n\n$$\n3({x}^{4}-16)=3({({x}^{2})}^{2}-{4}^{2})\n$$\n\nNow, we factor our expression using the difference of squares formula:\n\n$$\n3({({x}^{2})}^{2}-{4}^{2})=3({x}^{2}+4)({x}^{2}-4)\n$$\n\nFinally, we notice that the expression in the second set of parentheses is also a difference of squares. Therefore, the expression can be factored further, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3({x}^{2}+4)({x}^{2}-4) & =3({x}^{2}+4)({x}^{2}-{2}^{2}) \\\\  & =3({x}^{2}+4)(x+2)(x-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260712 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91bf0b47-327f-5b82-a87a-307b7eda9e1c" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Arithmetic Series Using Sigma Notation when Not All Terms are Shown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ee1979b5-07d0-5b55-8b52-d7cb7cea2780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(7-3n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260714}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ee1979b5-07d0-5b55-8b52-d7cb7cea2780" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(7-3n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260714} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc093138-0c56-5346-b7af-a7d27bd79a9d" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260713}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4b147fea-e6ff-5e9f-b32f-ff0ababdac80" :question/math-academy-id "q-5236" :question/problem "Express the following arithmetic series using sigma notation.\n\n$$\n4+1-2-…-20-23\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The first term ${a}_{1}=4$ and the common difference is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =1-(4) \\\\  & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =4+(n-1)(-3) \\\\  & =4-3n+3 \\\\  & =7-3n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe don't know the index of the last term, but we can find it using the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =-23 \\\\ 7-3n & =-23 \\\\ 7+23 & =3n \\\\ 30 & =3n \\\\ 10 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $10$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=10.$ So, the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(7-3n)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260718 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6813b06-c450-599f-9b64-0588ec4142d9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "817c3779-2936-58e7-a892-3e1f60da2e7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260720}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "817c3779-2936-58e7-a892-3e1f60da2e7f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260720} :answer-field/id #uuid "b530797f-f38a-5d88-8eb5-a1eb5d3c16a0" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260719}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ac22c321-341c-5b1a-8d5d-082d85416290" :question/math-academy-id "q-5300" :question/problem "What is the sum of the arithmetic series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}(-2n)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the total number of terms.\n\nGiven the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{8}(-2n)$ in sigma notation, we note that\n\n- the total number of terms is $N=8$\n\n- the first term is ${a}_{1}=-2\\cdot 1=-2$\n\n- the last term is ${a}_{8}=-2\\cdot 8=-16$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{8} & =\\frac{8}{2}(-2+(-16)) \\\\  & =4(-18) \\\\  & =-72\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260818 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fb53971d-15be-579e-86f5-4fc8d0c10d6d" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Arithmetic Series Using Sigma Notation when All Terms are Shown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33eeb54b-38bc-58d5-b90e-312ae4fb437e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}(6n-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260820}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33eeb54b-38bc-58d5-b90e-312ae4fb437e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}(6n-2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260820} :answer-field/id #uuid "876b68a7-80d5-5bc0-b0d3-a7e1849858a6" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260819}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "def295e5-6bc1-5660-bf4c-3c6ddeefa998" :question/math-academy-id "q-6447" :question/problem "The arithmetic series $4+10+16+22+28+34+40$ can be written in sigma notation as\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The first term is ${a}_{1}=4$ and the common difference is: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =10-4 \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =4+(n-1)(6) \\\\  & =4+6n-6 \\\\  & =6n-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $7$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=7.$ So the sigma notation for the given sum is:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}(6n-2)\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6813b06-c450-599f-9b64-0588ec4142d9" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given in Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "81c51b21-cf4d-54d7-8ea3-bf125558d5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260850}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81c51b21-cf4d-54d7-8ea3-bf125558d5d6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "105" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260850} :answer-field/id #uuid "39483401-c887-5eab-9097-84d46f3a7fff" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260849}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "51f91c45-43d2-5fcb-8a9f-a87826b50b9f" :question/math-academy-id "q-6491" :question/problem "What is the sum of the arithmetic series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(n+5)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the total number of terms.\n\nGiven the series ${\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(n+5)$ in sigma notation, we note that\n\n- the total number of terms is $N=10$\n\n- the first term is ${a}_{1}=1+5=6$\n\n- the last term is ${a}_{10}=10+5=15$\n\nSubstituting these values into the formula for the sum of an arithmetic series, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{10} & =\\frac{10}{2}(6+15) \\\\  & =5\\cdot 21 \\\\  & =105\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260872 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9c02e9f4-ac81-5fe7-8b37-3db5653b4693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9c02e9f4-ac81-5fe7-8b37-3db5653b4693" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260874} :answer-field/id #uuid "edd23c3b-c6ee-5b97-bbf0-41e4e3cfc8e8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260873}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "d9857563-42e2-5591-8956-ff251a3df14e" :question/math-academy-id "q-65551" :question/problem "Find the exact value of $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{13\\pi }{12}).$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{13\\pi }{12}=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$*\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x=\\frac{5\\pi }{4}$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{13\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{5\\pi }{4})\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4}-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{6}+\\sqrt{2}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260875 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "283415d8-47d7-5d42-8836-0c0b0c4efc85" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Values Using the Sum and Difference Formulas"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b20ccd39-3c8a-5cc2-ac4d-61d790088d67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b20ccd39-3c8a-5cc2-ac4d-61d790088d67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260877} :answer-field/id #uuid "206d5ba5-665d-57ac-a3f1-df6e1300a891" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260876}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7da7ff7e-6169-5165-9584-01d86caac744" :question/math-academy-id "q-65556" :question/problem "Find the exact value of  $\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{12}).$\n\n*Hint: Use the fact that $\\frac{\\pi }{12}=\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}.$*\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "Recall the difference formula for cosine:\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}(x-y)=\\operatorname{cos}x\\operatorname{cos}y+\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{sin}y\n$$\n\nApplying the difference formula for cosine with $x=\\frac{\\pi }{3}$ and $y=\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{12}) & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}-\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{4})+\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}+\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{4}+\\frac{\\sqrt{6}}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\sqrt{2}+\\sqrt{6}}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186260878 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5f9683d9-05b9-5a42-9f81-0170a15dfac1" :knowledge-point/title "Using the Double-Angle Formula to Simplify Ratios"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a100d4b9-e06d-544a-8102-9cebac72f6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260880}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a100d4b9-e06d-544a-8102-9cebac72f6d2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260880} :answer-field/id #uuid "6b99217f-d43a-5910-b990-0ce4f782f447" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186260879}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1e7013fe-76e9-5d3e-a584-12066788f47b" :question/math-academy-id "q-65608" :question/problem "$\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}=$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's recall the double-angle formula for sine: \n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}2x=2\\operatorname{sin}x\\operatorname{cos}x\n$$\n\nSubstituting $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}$ into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{6})=2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies as\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})=2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nDividing both sides by $\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{3})}{{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{{sin}^{2}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\operatorname{cos}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}{\\cancel{\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})}\\operatorname{sin}(\\frac{\\pi }{6})} \\\\  & =2\\operatorname{cot}(\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261001 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10095048-0db6-5b4e-889f-303f07449367" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions to Exponential Equations when Rearrangement is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "119305f0-b1fe-5b2f-8e3b-0d6015572b29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261003}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "119305f0-b1fe-5b2f-8e3b-0d6015572b29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261003} :answer-field/id #uuid "06df9466-5754-5c36-a3e3-d7675a140094" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261002}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c8d597e1-3a70-5551-8ca3-548ecbb3ddce" :question/math-academy-id "q-66901" :question/problem "Given that ${e}^{2h}-4=7,$ find the value of $h$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2h}-4 & =7 \\\\ {e}^{2h} & =11\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $h:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2h} & =11 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{2h}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(11) \\\\ 2h & =\\operatorname{ln}(11) \\\\ h & =\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(11)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $h=\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{ln}(11)$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\nh\\approx 1.199\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261004 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "10095048-0db6-5b4e-889f-303f07449367" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions to Exponential Equations when Rearrangement is Required"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8cc96152-4ed2-5310-8597-d190b5027215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261006}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8cc96152-4ed2-5310-8597-d190b5027215" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261006} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a59462f-4f5b-570c-b652-5baadec3debc" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261005}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6e5cb6a2-7bc8-5f55-bf93-6f94f39a879f" :question/math-academy-id "q-66904" :question/problem "Given that ${e}^{-7p}+3=8,$ find the value of $p$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we rearrange our equation into a form that is more convenient for logarithms, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-7p}+3 & =8 \\\\ {e}^{-7p} & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation and solve for $p:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{-7p} & =5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{-7p}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(5) \\\\ -7p & =\\operatorname{ln}(5) \\\\ p & =-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $p=-\\frac{1}{7}\\operatorname{ln}(5)$\n\nEvaluating the above expression using a calculator gives\n\n$$\np\\approx -0.230\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261010 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "4d8c6c93-9d15-5482-8130-25bb9cabf008" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions to Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "547d5c88-680a-58d0-88fd-be47bbc9dd96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}7-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261012}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "547d5c88-680a-58d0-88fd-be47bbc9dd96" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}7-\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261012} :answer-field/id #uuid "61dd824c-1395-5163-a6e9-bb2bbece39d8" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261011}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "a4fcecca-42f0-5b23-b6e5-a78929f1c209" :question/math-academy-id "q-66963" :question/problem "What is the value of $x$ if ${e}^{2x+3}=\\sqrt[5]{7}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x+3} & =\\sqrt[5]{7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}({e}^{2x+3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}(\\sqrt[5]{7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\n2x+3=\\operatorname{ln}(\\sqrt[5]{7})\n$$\n\nNext, we write $\\sqrt[5]{7}$ as ${7}^{1/5}.$ So, we get:\n\n$$\n2x+3=\\operatorname{ln}({7}^{1/5})\n$$\n\nFinally, we rewrite the right-hand side using power law of logarithms and solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x+3 & =\\operatorname{ln}({7}^{1/5}) \\\\ 2x+3 & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}(7) \\\\ 2x & =\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}(7)-3 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{2}(\\frac{1}{5}\\operatorname{ln}(7)-3) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}7-\\frac{3}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $x=\\frac{1}{10}\\operatorname{ln}7-\\frac{3}{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261013 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "829ac8f1-7ade-5dd9-9f2f-78234a57a257" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given the First and Last Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "45bf49dd-7841-5382-a86f-093a0a3c4e69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-273" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261015}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "45bf49dd-7841-5382-a86f-093a0a3c4e69" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-273" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261015} :answer-field/id #uuid "878d60a5-6709-5ede-b238-4483f961cae3" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261014}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1b0d02cf-7e0a-5619-ba74-9c01c7f880f0" :question/math-academy-id "q-67043" :question/problem "The following arithmetic series has $13$ terms in total. Find the sum of the series.\n\n$$\n-9+(-11)+(-13)+(-15)+⋯+(-33)\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nFor our series, we have ${a}_{1}=-9,{a}_{N}=-33$ and $N=13.$ Substituting these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{13} & =\\frac{13}{2}(-9+(-33)) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}(-42) \\\\  & =13(-21) \\\\  & =-273\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261016 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "829ac8f1-7ade-5dd9-9f2f-78234a57a257" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given the First and Last Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4eddb648-ec6d-570d-9987-b5a58e4f4ce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "330" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261018}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4eddb648-ec6d-570d-9987-b5a58e4f4ce2" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "330" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261018} :answer-field/id #uuid "67ab2d8d-687a-57cc-bd4e-e89fb5e0950a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261017}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4fced0be-707f-5fb0-9e9e-ae88a44c183d" :question/math-academy-id "q-67047" :question/problem "The following arithmetic series has $15$ terms in total. Find the sum of the series.\n\n$$\n-6+(-2)+2+6+⋯+50\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}({a}_{1}+{a}_{N}),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, ${a}_{N}$ is the last term, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nFor our series, we have ${a}_{1}=-6,{a}_{N}=50$ and $N=15.$ Substituting these values into the formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{15} & =\\frac{15}{2}(-6+50) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot 44 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 22 \\\\  & =330\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261025 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6133cdf6-6931-5363-918f-5ca8cd97b732" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given Two Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ceaf749a-0891-5b69-b7ba-0e8416117672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261027}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ceaf749a-0891-5b69-b7ba-0e8416117672" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "52" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261027} :answer-field/id #uuid "a9f1ca61-45bb-5af2-b0d1-664076cee0bb" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261026}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "6a41ce80-b8e8-5930-a1cd-1406aa9952fe" :question/math-academy-id "q-67177" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has ${a}_{1}=1$ and ${a}_{5}=3,$ where ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{5}$ are the first and fifth terms of the series, respectively. What is the sum of the first $13$ terms of the series?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nWe know that ${a}_{5}=3.$ So, using the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{5} & ={a}_{1}+(5-1)d \\\\ 3 & =1+4d \\\\ 4d & =2 \\\\ d & =\\frac{1}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, substituting ${a}_{1}=1$, $d=\\frac{1}{2}$, and $N=13$ into the above formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{13} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}(2\\cdot 1+(13-1)\\cdot \\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}\\cdot (2+6) \\\\  & =\\frac{13}{2}\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =13\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =52\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261028 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6133cdf6-6931-5363-918f-5ca8cd97b732" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Sum of an Arithmetic Series Given Two Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ae38c78a-c742-5fee-b66d-6fd8a1173707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-405" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261030}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ae38c78a-c742-5fee-b66d-6fd8a1173707" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-405" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261030} :answer-field/id #uuid "ec0376b2-78bc-5aa1-867c-d5690b732c26" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261029}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "32b30204-fdd2-5361-b71f-01c0c6f85c10" :question/math-academy-id "q-67183" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has ${a}_{4}=-11$ and ${a}_{11}=-39,$ where ${a}_{4}$ and ${a}_{11}$ are the fourth and eleventh terms of the series, respectively. \nFind the sum of the first $15$ terms of the series.\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "To calculate the sum of an arithmetic series, we use the formula\n\n$$\n{S}_{N}=\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d),\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ is the first term, $d$ is the common difference, and $N$ is the number of terms.\n\nSince we know two terms of the arithmetic series, we can find its common difference, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & =\\frac{{a}_{11}-{a}_{4}}{11-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-39-(-11)}{11-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{-28}{7} \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, as ${a}_{4}=-11$, using the formula for the $n$th term, we can compute ${a}_{1}$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ {a}_{4} & ={a}_{1}+(4-1)\\cdot (-4) \\\\ -11 & ={a}_{1}+(-12) \\\\ {a}_{1} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${a}_{1}=1$, $d=-4$, and $N=15$ into the above formula for ${S}_{N}$, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{S}_{15} & =\\frac{N}{2}(2{a}_{1}+(N-1)d) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}(2\\cdot 1+(15-1)\\cdot (-4)) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot (2-56) \\\\  & =\\frac{15}{2}\\cdot (-54) \\\\  & =15\\cdot (-27) \\\\  & =-405\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261046 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e551c82-6984-5246-9e74-73fa3b55ffe1" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Arithmetic Series Using Sigma Notation Given Two Terms of the Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d729dfb7-37c2-5721-b7da-0dc8d6f93eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-3n+13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261048}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d729dfb7-37c2-5721-b7da-0dc8d6f93eb9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-3n+13)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261048} :answer-field/id #uuid "a97607db-c43b-584f-8802-45c2e2e2bd33" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261047}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "37dec902-223c-5579-835f-ac0779fdd1ed" :question/math-academy-id "q-67281" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has ${a}_{1}=10$ and ${a}_{6}=-5,$ where ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{6}$ are the first and sixth terms of the series, respectively. What is the sum of the first ten terms of the series, expressed using sigma notation?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We know that ${a}_{6}=-5.$ So, using the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ -5 & =10+(6-1)d \\\\ -5 & =10+5d \\\\ 5d & =-15 \\\\ d & =-3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${a}_{1}=10$ and $d=-3.$ Thus, the formula for the $n$th term is:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =10+(n-1)(-3) \\\\  & =10-3n+3 \\\\  & =-3n+13\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, the sum of the first ten terms can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-3n+13)\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261052 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6532d0fb-1a24-5177-bd20-2efcb3e13f3d" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series With a Negative Common Ratio Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5d17aa3c-2d49-5537-99b1-465928ad76af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261054}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5d17aa3c-2d49-5537-99b1-465928ad76af" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261054} :answer-field/id #uuid "330a5f64-318d-5fec-87c8-1b28e2989524" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261053}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a2cca1fe-4d6f-58e1-ae4a-bc0f765dbc4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-67324" :question/problem "The geometric series $-2+12-72+432$ can be expressed using sigma notation as\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=-2$ and $N=4.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we calculate the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{12}{-2}=-6\n$$\n\nTherefore, expressing the given geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2+12-72+432 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (-6)^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify the above using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (-6)^{n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (-6)^{n}\\cdot (-6)^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(-2)\\cdot {(-6)}^{-1}\\cdot (-6)^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}(-2)\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{6})(-6)^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{4}\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (-6)^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261058 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "9f94b20f-9190-5851-ba54-e7687a223a41" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series Using Sigma Notation With Simplifications"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f251cd0d-defd-5842-9f71-c73f345f8fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-\\frac{5}{6}){6}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261060}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f251cd0d-defd-5842-9f71-c73f345f8fbc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-\\frac{5}{6}){6}^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261060} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a1bccbf-536d-5d16-b079-a27057fb73fe" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261059}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "02a78a86-1025-5579-a281-d9673722caa0" :question/math-academy-id "q-67377" :question/problem "The first term of a geometric sequence is $-5$ and the common ratio is $6.$ Written in sigma notation, what is the sum of the first $22$ terms?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we must express the series in the form:\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=-5,$ $r=6$ and $N=22.$\n\nExpressing the geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n{S}_{22}={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-5)\\cdot {6}^{n-1}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify the above using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-5)\\cdot {6}^{n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-5)\\cdot {6}^{n}\\cdot {6}^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-5)\\cdot {6}^{-1}\\cdot {6}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-5)\\cdot \\frac{1}{6}\\cdot {6}^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{22}(-\\frac{5}{6}){6}^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261289 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de7d614-76c9-5133-a4df-95f079467cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality With No Constant Term Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c47231ee-7d20-5dcb-abd4-da4f4bd0bf2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261291}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c47231ee-7d20-5dcb-abd4-da4f4bd0bf2b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [0,\\infty )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261291} :answer-field/id #uuid "5dde4dc9-69b0-5067-9339-80353b4c536a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261290}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "2f8458e8-0bfe-5745-a2d8-3bb1285a95ef" :question/math-academy-id "q-70921" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is ${x}^{2}+3x\\ge 0?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+3x & \\ge 0 \\\\ x(x+3) & \\ge 0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $x(x+3)=0.$ The roots are $x=-3$ and $x=0,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,-3),$ $(-3,0),$ and $(0,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-3)$ | $(-3,0)$ | $(0,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $x+3$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x(x+3)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(-)\\cdot (+)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $x(x+3)\\ge 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $x(x+3)$ is zero or positive.\n\nWe see that the expression is positive on the intervals $(-\\infty ,-3)$ and $(0,\\infty ).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=-3$ and $x=0.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that ${x}^{2}+3x\\ge 0$ for all $x\\in (-\\infty ,-3]\\cup [0,\\infty ).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261292 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "7de7d614-76c9-5133-a4df-95f079467cb9" :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Quadratic Inequality With No Constant Term Using the Sign Table Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39836889-10e4-5f79-a236-f8a552a94d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [0,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261294}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39836889-10e4-5f79-a236-f8a552a94d9a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x\\in [0,2]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261294} :answer-field/id #uuid "7418a723-891c-5517-b74d-7d070f5ede1a" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261293}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "7ac8026b-ced5-5ad5-9d67-ebf801b0d418" :question/math-academy-id "q-70963" :question/problem "For which values of $x$ is $3{x}^{2}-6x\\le 0?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, let's factor the left-hand side of the inequality:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3{x}^{2}-6x & \\le 0 \\\\ 3x(x-2) & \\le 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the roots of the corresponding equation $3x(x-2)=0.$ The roots are $x=0$ and $x=2,$ and they split the number line into the $3$ intervals $(-\\infty ,0),$ $(0,2),$ and $(2,\\infty ).$\n\nNow, we construct a sign table with the list of the intervals on the top and the list of factors on the left. Filling in the sign table using the usual method, we get the following result:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,0)$ | $(0,2)$ | $(2,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $x-2$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $3x(x-2)$ | $(-)\\cdot (-)=+$ | $(+)\\cdot (-)=-$ | $(+)\\cdot (+)=+$ |\n\nFinally, to solve the quadratic inequality $3x(x-2)\\le 0,$ we look at the bottom row to find out where the expression $3x(x-2)$ is zero or negative.\n\nWe see that the expression is negative on the interval $(0,2).$ Also, we know that the expression equals $0$ at the points $x=0$ and $x=2.$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that $3{x}^{2}-6x\\le 0$ for all $x\\in [0,2].$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261368 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "0fa5bdf9-4b27-5565-9ee1-ecb9f9db4791" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Units of a Derivative"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "79cd8330-ab97-5903-b9f3-ef508c00087b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dollars per point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261370}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "79cd8330-ab97-5903-b9f3-ef508c00087b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "Dollars per point" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261370} :answer-field/id #uuid "3810100f-eb13-58ea-9c35-58a116899722" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261369}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dbe3b6c2-cec5-5e7c-9393-5197f7a69152" :question/math-academy-id "q-72091" :question/problem "A student's parents promise to reward her $R(p)$ dollars if she obtains an average of $p$ points in her exams. What are the units of $\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nWrite the units in the form \"[output units] per [input unit]\"." :question/worked-solution "We have that the student's reward $R(p)$ is measured in dollars and the average exam result is measured in points. \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}R(p): & \\text{dollars} \\\\ p: & \\text{points}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe derivative $\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}$ measures a change in $R(p)$ over a change in $p.$ So, it represents a ratio of the units:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}: & \\frac{\\text{dollars}}{\\text{points}}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn other words, $\\frac{\\text{d}R}{\\text{d}p}$ is measured in \"dollars per point\"."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261371 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "fa38f112-466c-50e9-ac08-be72b6f53782" :knowledge-point/title "Relating a Derivative to a Rate of Change"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2198cbf6-3358-5668-b855-4d284e8f7fa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B'(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261373}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2198cbf6-3358-5668-b855-4d284e8f7fa4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "B'(t)=r(t)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261373} :answer-field/id #uuid "44e0582e-f1b4-5db2-841e-f70823d9729b" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261372}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "aa386819-df2b-519d-bbb5-a033b05e50c2" :question/math-academy-id "q-72109" :question/problem "Ramin, a bricklayer, is monitoring the construction of a wall by recording the following two numbers for different values of $t,$ where $t$ is the time, in hours, since he started his observations:\n\n- $B(t)$ - the number of bricks in the wall.\n\n- $r(t)$ - the rate at which the number of bricks in the wall is increasing.\n\nWhat is the relationship between $B(t)$ and $r(t)?$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The function $B(t)$ gives the number of bricks inside the wall after $t$ hours. Then,\n\n$$\n{B}^{'}(t)=\\frac{\\text{d}B(t)}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{Change in }B}{\\text{Change in }t}.\n$$\n\nThus, ${B}^{'}(t)$ gives the rate at which the number of bricks increases at time $t$, which is the same as $r(t).$ Therefore, the relationship between $B(t)$ and $r(t)$ is ${B}^{'}(t)=r(t).$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261495 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "91bf0b47-327f-5b82-a87a-307b7eda9e1c" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Arithmetic Series Using Sigma Notation when Not All Terms are Shown"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18e8c764-23b0-5d28-9947-82d06b2ffc58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}(6-\\frac{1}{2}n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261497}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18e8c764-23b0-5d28-9947-82d06b2ffc58" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}(6-\\frac{1}{2}n)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261497} :answer-field/id #uuid "7fa66176-f8bd-589b-9b30-7f8f36fef209" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261496}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "06651c53-f1bd-5c95-9ed0-46ecf11e946a" :question/math-academy-id "q-73302" :question/problem "Express the following arithmetic series using sigma notation.\n\n$$\n\\frac{11}{2}+5+\\frac{9}{2}+…+0\n$$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "The first term ${a}_{1}=\\frac{11}{2}$ and the common difference is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}d & ={a}_{2}-{a}_{1} \\\\  & =5-\\frac{11}{2} \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the formula for the $n$th term is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}+(n-1)(-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{11}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}n+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =6-\\frac{1}{2}n.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe don't know the index of the last term, but we can find it using the formula for the $n$th term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & =0 \\\\ 6-\\frac{1}{2}n & =0 \\\\ 6 & =\\frac{1}{2}n \\\\ 12 & =n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are $12$ terms in total, from $n=1$ to $n=12.$ So, the sigma notation for the given sum is\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{12}(6-\\frac{1}{2}n).\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261498 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "5e551c82-6984-5246-9e74-73fa3b55ffe1" :knowledge-point/title "Writing an Arithmetic Series Using Sigma Notation Given Two Terms of the Series"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9d4f459f-4f9f-558c-ad05-c93e80267855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-2n+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261500}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9d4f459f-4f9f-558c-ad05-c93e80267855" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-2n+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261500} :answer-field/id #uuid "f47d8c72-c7b9-5879-a4c1-4f6358c3fe54" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261499}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "342b667a-994a-57b7-a61a-0660abedb286" :question/math-academy-id "q-73303" :question/problem "An arithmetic series has ${a}_{1}=3$ and ${a}_{5}=-5,$ where ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{5}$ are the first and fifth terms of the series, respectively. What is the sum of the first ten terms of the series, expressed using sigma notation?\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We know that ${a}_{5}=-5.$ So, using the formula for the $n$th term of an arithmetic sequence, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\ -5 & =3+(5-1)d \\\\ -5 & =3+4d \\\\ 4d & =-8 \\\\ d & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${a}_{1}=3$ and $d=-2.$ Thus, the formula for the $n$th term is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{n} & ={a}_{1}+(n-1)d \\\\  & =3+(n-1)(-2) \\\\  & =3-2n+2 \\\\  & =-2n+5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally then, the sum of the first ten terms can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{10}(-2n+5).\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186261766 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "b18ace11-d31c-5d5a-bd2e-32e1c28af2b9" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Vertical Asymptotes When the Denominator Has Complex Roots"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "58eeb9bd-8760-51d2-b2d7-e15ea6490c9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261768}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "58eeb9bd-8760-51d2-b2d7-e15ea6490c9b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No vertical asymptotes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261768} :answer-field/id #uuid "878de882-ea49-5dfb-8a79-fdd6fee775dd" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186261767}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "94f25a88-826e-5f93-a9c5-c2932f446753" :question/math-academy-id "q-80680" :question/problem "Calculate the vertical asymptotes of $f(x)=\\frac{x+1}{{x}^{2}+3x+4}.$\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}\n\nIf none exist, enter a phrase such as \"No vertical asymptotes\"." :question/worked-solution "To find the vertical asymptotes of a rational function, we factor the numerator and denominator and cancel any common factors. Then, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for $x:$\n\nNote that the equation ${x}^{2}+3x+4=0$ has no real solutions because the discriminant $D$ is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={3}^{2}-4(1)(4) \\\\  & =9-16 \\\\  & =-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $y=f(x)$ has no vertical asymptotes."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262103 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6532d0fb-1a24-5177-bd20-2efcb3e13f3d" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series With a Negative Common Ratio Using Sigma Notation"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e6d39e35-0e22-5cda-8e8a-0f38f7efee86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262105}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e6d39e35-0e22-5cda-8e8a-0f38f7efee86" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{n}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262105} :answer-field/id #uuid "fd78fcee-b881-529b-8713-eee28822801e" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262104}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b1790c68-9a6b-5d0b-85dc-5ef421b499cd" :question/math-academy-id "q-9755" :question/problem "The geometric series $-1+2-4+8-16+32\\,$ can be expressed using sigma notation as\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=-1$ and $N=6.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we calculate the ratio of the first two terms in the series:\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{2}{-1}=-2\n$$\n\nHence, expressing the geometric series given in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n-1+2-4+8-16+32={\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(-1)\\cdot (-2)^{n-1}\\,\\,\n$$\n\nFinally, we can simplify the above using the laws of exponents, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(-1)\\cdot (-2)^{n-1} & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}(-1)\\cdot (-2)^{n}\\cdot (-2)^{-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}{2}^{-1}\\cdot (-2)^{n} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{6}\\frac{1}{2}(-2)^{n}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186262106 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "57e44759-4db8-57e3-bfb4-ce026d4d73b2" :knowledge-point/title "Writing a Geometric Series Using Sigma Notation by Calculating the Number of Terms"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e068698-f473-5474-92fe-df8f66a8ecc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}{4}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262108}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1e068698-f473-5474-92fe-df8f66a8ecc1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}{4}^{n-1}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262108} :answer-field/id #uuid "d564bbb1-493d-52a4-b0b6-eeeb3e75f95f" :answer-field/key "answer" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186262107}] :question/difficulty {:db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2509ffde-6c9d-5b43-bb99-17cd7228a183" :question/math-academy-id "q-9760" :question/problem "Write the geometric series $1+4+16+⋯+4\\,096$ using sigma notation.\n\n*Hint: You may wish to make use of the fact that $4\\,096={4}^{6}.$*\n\nAnswer: {{answer}}" :question/worked-solution "We must express the given series in the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{N}{a}_{1}{r}^{n-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${a}_{1}$ denotes the first term of the series, $r$ denotes the common ratio, and $N$ denotes the number of terms in the series. In this case, ${a}_{1}=1.$\n\nTo find $r,$ we find the ratio of the consecutive terms ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}:$\n\n$$\nr=\\frac{{a}_{2}}{{a}_{1}}=\\frac{4}{1}=4\n$$\n\nNow we must determine $N$ (the index of the last term). To do this, we use the fact that\n\n$$\n{a}_{N}={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1}.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{a}_{N} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{N-1} \\\\ 4\\,096 & ={4}^{N-1} \\\\ {4}^{6} & ={4}^{N-1} \\\\ 6 & =N-1 \\\\ N & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nExpressing the geometric series in sigma notation, we obtain:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1+4+16+⋯+4\\,096 & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}1\\cdot {4}^{n-1} \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{n=1}^{7}{4}^{n-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
